QUESTIONS.push(
/* ---------------- Preventing absorption revisited ---------------- */
{id:"L16-001", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"absorbrev", skill:"apply", readingId:"dipiro-e9",
 concept:"charcoal-best-by-outcomes", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Of the methods used to prevent absorption of an ingested poison (emesis, gastric lavage, whole bowel irrigation, activated charcoal), which one does the patient-outcome data support best?",
 options:[
  {t:"Activated charcoal", correct:true, why:"Based on patient outcomes, charcoal is probably the best option; the caveat is that not everything a person ingests can be grabbed by charcoal, and charcoal has its own risk."},
  {t:"Emesis", correct:false, why:"Emesis is no longer recommended; aspiration is its biggest risk."},
  {t:"Gastric lavage", correct:false, why:"Lavage is risky; an emergency team might still try it when someone has really overdosed and might not otherwise be saved."},
  {t:"Whole bowel irrigation", correct:false, why:"Irrigation is one of the options an experienced team might try, but it is not the one the outcome data favor."},
  {t:"All four are equally supported by outcome data", correct:false, why:"The outcome data, collected from treated cases and case series rather than trials, single out charcoal (single-dose, and multiple-dose for some compounds) as working best. Emesis, lavage and irrigation each carry risk and are still tried mainly in significant overdoses."}],
 teach:"The outcome data on treating poisoned patients come from cases that were treated and then written up, not from clinical trials. Those data show that charcoal, single-dose and for some compounds multiple-dose, improves patient outcomes best among the absorption-prevention methods. Two caveats: not every compound sticks to charcoal, and charcoal has its own risk.",
 quote:"Yeah, so charcoal, based on patient outcomes is probably your best option. But not everything people ingest can be grabbed by charcoal in their stomach, right?",
 note:"A 10/19 addition to the 10/14 discussion of the DiPiro section (L15-059 to L15-062): the ranking by outcome data was new.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment More on preventing absorption’ (DiPiro ch. e9); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-002", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"absorbrev", skill:"apply",
 concept:"charcoal-time-window-reason", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Single-dose activated charcoal is given within about 2 hours of an ingestion. Why does it work poorly if it is given 4 to 6 hours later?",
 options:[
  {t:"The poison has moved on out of the stomach and small intestine", correct:true, why:"Charcoal must be in the stomach and small intestine while the poison is still there; an orally ingested poison moves on through."},
  {t:"Stomach acid inactivates the charcoal after a few hours", correct:false, why:"The limit is where the poison is, not the charcoal's stability."},
  {t:"The charcoal itself is absorbed into the blood by then", correct:false, why:"Charcoal is not absorbed; it leaves in the feces with whatever it bound."},
  {t:"Enterohepatic recirculation of the poison stops after the first 2 hours", correct:false, why:"Recirculation is the reason for multiple-dose charcoal, not the reason for the single-dose window."},
  {t:"The patient is too sedated to swallow it by then", correct:false, why:"The window is set by where the poison is, not by the patient's state: an orally ingested poison keeps moving through the gut, so after a few hours little is left in the stomach and small intestine for the charcoal to bind."}],
 teach:"The window for single-dose charcoal used to be an hour and has been lengthened to about 2 hours. The reason there is a window at all is location: the ingested poison keeps moving down the gut, so the charcoal has to reach the stomach and small intestine while the poison is still there. Multiple-dose charcoal is the separate case for compounds that stick to charcoal and are re-injected into the small intestine through the bile.",
 quote:"Yeah, up to 2 hours. They used to say just an hour, but they've, they've lengthened that a little bit, maybe up to 2 hours, OK? ... Why does it not work so well if you wait 4 to 6 hours to give them charcoal? They've ingested it orally. Stuff moves on through, right? So you need to get that charcoal in their stomach and small intestine while the poison is still in that area of the body, OK?",
 note:"The 1-to-2-hour window is L15-060; the reason for the window was added on 10/19.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Prevent Absorption’; transcript 10/19"},

/* ---------------- Inhibit bioactivation ---------------- */
{id:"L16-003", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"bioact", skill:"recall",
 concept:"inhibit-bioactivation-meaning", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"As a toxicokinetic treatment, what does inhibiting bioactivation mean, and how often can it be done?",
 options:[
  {t:"Blocking the enzyme that makes the toxic metabolite; rarely possible", correct:true, why:"Bioactivation is metabolism to something more noxious; the treatment inhibits the enzymes involved, and there are not many cases where this can be done."},
  {t:"Inducing the enzymes that detoxify the poison; the most common approach", correct:false, why:"Induction takes days and is not possible in a poisoning; that is the enhance-metabolism story anyway."},
  {t:"Binding the poison in the gut so that it is never absorbed; used for most ingestions", correct:false, why:"That is preventing absorption."},
  {t:"Pulling the toxic metabolite off its target enzyme; possible for every poison", correct:false, why:"Removal from the active site is a pharmacological treatment, and it is not possible for every poison."},
  {t:"Acidifying the urine so the metabolite is trapped; used for weak bases", correct:false, why:"Ion trapping is enhancing renal elimination, and it is not done for weak bases."}],
 teach:"Bioactivation is metabolism that changes what was ingested into something more poisonous. The toxicokinetic treatment is to inhibit the enzymes doing that conversion so the toxic metabolite is never made. It is possible in only a few cases; methanol and ethylene glycol are the classic examples used to teach it.",
 quote:"There are not many cases where we can do this, where we can actually prevent the metabolism to a more toxic metabolite, but these are some classic examples.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Inhibit Bioactivation’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-004", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"bioact", skill:"apply",
 concept:"methanol-formic-acid-retina", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient has drunk methanol. Which metabolite causes the harm, and what does it damage?",
 options:[
  {t:"Formic acid; the retina, causing loss of sight", correct:true, why:"Methanol goes to formaldehyde, then formaldehyde dehydrogenase makes formic acid, the toxic product; it kills the receptors at the back of the retina."},
  {t:"Formaldehyde; the liver", correct:false, why:"Formaldehyde is the intermediate; the toxic compound she named is formic acid, and the target is the eye."},
  {t:"Oxalic acid; the microvasculature of the kidneys and liver", correct:false, why:"Oxalic acid is the ethylene glycol end product."},
  {t:"Glycolic acid; the heart", correct:false, why:"Glycolic acid is on the ethylene glycol path."},
  {t:"Methanol itself; the lungs", correct:false, why:"The parent compound is bioactivated; the metabolite does the damage."}],
 teach:"Methanol is metabolized by alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde, then by formaldehyde dehydrogenase to formic acid. Formic acid is the toxic product of the bioactivation: it damages the retina, so loss of sight is the symptom associated with methanol poisoning.",
 quote:"Then it goes through another dehydrogenase, the formaldehyde dehydrogenase, and it forms formic acid. This is the compound in the bioactivation of methanol that is toxic. Formic acid will cause damage, especially to the eyes, to the retina of the eyes. It'll, it'll kill those receptors in the back of the retina, and therefore you'll lose sight.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: methanol and ethylene glycol examples’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-005", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"bioact", skill:"figure", type:"match",
 concept:"methanol-eg-pathways", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Match each step of the methanol and ethylene glycol bioactivation pathways in the figure with the compound formed.",
 left:["Methanol, by alcohol dehydrogenase", "Formaldehyde, by formaldehyde dehydrogenase", "Ethylene glycol, by alcohol dehydrogenase", "Glycolic acid, after several more steps"],
 right:["Formaldehyde", "Formic acid (toxic to the retina)", "Glycoaldehyde", "Oxalic acid (forms microcrystals)"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Methanol, by alcohol dehydrogenase", r:"Formaldehyde", why:"The first step of the methanol path."},
  {l:"Formaldehyde, by formaldehyde dehydrogenase", r:"Formic acid (toxic to the retina)", why:"The toxic end product of methanol."},
  {l:"Ethylene glycol, by alcohol dehydrogenase", r:"Glycoaldehyde", why:"The first step of the ethylene glycol path; aldehyde dehydrogenase then makes glycolic acid."},
  {l:"Glycolic acid, after several more steps", r:"Oxalic acid (forms microcrystals)", why:"Oxalic acid precipitates in small vessels and damages the kidneys and liver."}],
 teach:"Both alcohols share the first enzyme, alcohol dehydrogenase. Methanol: alcohol dehydrogenase makes formaldehyde, formaldehyde dehydrogenase makes formic acid. Ethylene glycol: alcohol dehydrogenase makes glycoaldehyde, aldehyde dehydrogenase makes glycolic acid, and several more steps give oxalic acid. The shared first enzyme is why one antidote serves both.",
 quote:"It's going from methanol. It gets metabolized by alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde. ... it is metabolized again by, uh, alcohol dehydrogenase to glycoaldehyde, and then it goes through. Aldehyde dehydrogenase to glycolic acid, then it takes several more steps and it goes to oxalic acid, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: methanol and ethylene glycol examples’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-006", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"bioact", skill:"apply",
 concept:"ethylene-glycol-oxalic-crystals", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why is ethylene glycol dangerous once it has been metabolized?",
 options:[
  {t:"Oxalic acid crystals block small vessels in the kidneys and liver", correct:true, why:"Oxalic acid, a small molecule, precipitates in the blood as microcrystals that block blood flow in the small vessels of organs and damage them."},
  {t:"Glycoaldehyde destroys the retina and causes blindness", correct:false, why:"Blindness is the methanol story, from formic acid."},
  {t:"The parent compound is caustic and blows apart the gut wall the way iron does", correct:false, why:"Ethylene glycol tastes sweet and does not burn going down."},
  {t:"It is converted to formic acid, which stops cellular respiration", correct:false, why:"Formic acid comes from methanol, and stopping respiration is the cyanide mechanism."},
  {t:"It inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase, so ethanol accumulates", correct:false, why:"Ethylene glycol is a substrate of alcohol dehydrogenase; inhibiting that enzyme is the treatment."}],
 teach:"Ethylene glycol ends up as oxalic acid. Oxalic acid sometimes precipitates in the blood into small crystals; in the microvasculature of organs such as the liver and kidneys those crystals block blood flow and damage the organ. That is why an exposure that tastes sweet and does not burn is a significant poisoning.",
 quote:"Well, oxalic acid is a, is a small molecule, and, um, uh, it will sometimes precipitate in the blood to form small microcrystals and The impact of that is you can damage your organs like your liver, your kidneys.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: methanol and ethylene glycol examples’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-007", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"bioact", skill:"apply",
 concept:"ethylene-glycol-exposure-source", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Ethylene glycol is a common accidental exposure. Which pair gives its usual source and the reason cats and small children swallow it?",
 options:[
  {t:"Antifreeze; it smells and tastes sweet", correct:true, why:"A leak under a car leaves antifreeze on the driveway; it does not burn going down and tastes sweet, so a cat may lick it up and a small child may put it in the mouth."},
  {t:"Antifreeze; its pink or blue color attracts them", correct:false, why:"The color is a dye added to antifreeze; ethylene glycol itself has no color, and the attraction is the sweet taste."},
  {t:"Windshield washer fluid; it looks like water", correct:false, why:"Ethylene glycol is the antifreeze in cars. What draws pets and small children is not that it looks like water but that it smells and tastes sweet and does not burn going down."},
  {t:"Rubbing alcohol; it smells like ethanol", correct:false, why:"The common ethylene glycol exposure is antifreeze leaking from a car onto the driveway. Pets and children swallow it because it smells and tastes sweet, not because of any resemblance to ethanol."},
  {t:"Drain cleaner; it is odorless", correct:false, why:"Ethylene glycol is not odorless: it smells and tastes sweet, which is exactly why a cat licks up an antifreeze leak and a small child swallows it."}],
 teach:"Ethylene glycol is antifreeze. The pink or blue of a driveway spot is a dye, not the compound. It smells and tastes sweet and does not burn going down, so pets lick up leaks and small children, who put everything in their mouths, get exposed while playing outside. Methanol exposures are different: most are people with an alcohol use disorder who cannot get ethanol and drink methanol instead.",
 quote:"anybody can think of what we use in our cars that's made of ethylene glycol? Antifreeze, right? ... It smells sweet. You've never gone down to sniff it. I'm just letting you know, it smells and tastes sweet. OK, so it's a problem for animals. ... the methanol exposure, most of the time, those are people that have an alcohol substance use disorder.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: methanol and ethylene glycol examples’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-008", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"bioact", skill:"drug",
 concept:"methanol-eg-antidote-adh", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is the antidote for methanol and for ethylene glycol poisoning, and how does it work?",
 options:[
  {t:"Ethanol or fomepizole; both inhibit alcohol dehydrogenase", correct:true, why:"Ethanol outcompetes the alcohol dehydrogenase metabolism of methanol or ethylene glycol; fomepizole binds to and inhibits the enzyme; either way the toxic metabolites are not made."},
  {t:"N-acetylcysteine; it supplies the ingredients for glutathione", correct:false, why:"That is the acetaminophen treatment."},
  {t:"Sodium bicarbonate; it traps the acid metabolites in the urine", correct:false, why:"Bicarbonate is ion trapping for salicylates and phenobarbital, not the antidote here."},
  {t:"Methylene blue; it reduces methemoglobin back to hemoglobin", correct:false, why:"Methylene blue belongs to the cyanide scheme."},
  {t:"Dimercaprol; it chelates the alcohol", correct:false, why:"Chelators bind metals, not alcohols."}],
 teach:"Both poisons are bioactivated by alcohol dehydrogenase, so one antidote serves both: ethanol, which competes for the enzyme, or fomepizole, which binds and inhibits it. With the enzyme blocked, methanol is not turned into formic acid and ethylene glycol is not turned into oxalic acid. This is the classic example of a toxicokinetic treatment that blocks bioactivation.",
 quote:"there's an antidote for methanol and ethylene glycol, and you can use ethanol or now a drug called omeprazole. So, you can give ethanol. And outcompete the alcohol alcohol dehydrogenase metabolism of methanol or ethylene glycol. Same thing with fomeprazole. It'll, it'll bind to and inhibit the alcohol dehydrogenase and prevent the metabolism of methanol or ethylene glycol.",
 note:"The transcript renders fomepizole as \"omeprazole\" and \"fomeprazole\"; the slide says \"Antidote for both is ethanol or fomepizole.\" Keyed on fomepizole, the drug on the slide and the one she describes (an alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor); omeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor and is a transcription error.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: methanol and ethylene glycol examples’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-009", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"bioact", skill:"tell",
 concept:"ethanol-vs-fomepizole", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Emergency rooms once kept a bottle of Everclear for methanol and ethylene glycol poisonings. How did that treatment work, and why is fomepizole used now?",
 options:[
  {t:"Ethanol competes for the enzyme; fomepizole is stronger, with fewer adverse effects", correct:true, why:"A shot or two of high-proof ethanol outcompeted alcohol dehydrogenase. Her key: fomepizole is the stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor and has fewer adverse effects than ethanol; on 10/19 she added better control of the dosing."},
  {t:"Ethanol induces the enzyme; fomepizole inhibits it", correct:false, why:"Both block the metabolism; ethanol does so by competition, not induction."},
  {t:"Ethanol works only for methanol poisoning; fomepizole works only for ethylene glycol poisoning", correct:false, why:"Either agent serves both poisons; the enzyme is the same."},
  {t:"Ethanol reverses the blindness; fomepizole prevents it", correct:false, why:"Neither reverses retinal damage; both prevent formic acid from being made."},
  {t:"Both chelate the toxic acid metabolites", correct:false, why:"Neither is a chelator; they act on the enzyme before the metabolites form."}],
 teach:"In the old days an emergency room kept Everclear, ethanol with a high percent by volume, and gave a poisoned patient a shot or two to outcompete alcohol dehydrogenase; she called that unrefined. Fomepizole is the prescription alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor used now, with better control of dosing. Her table key: fomepizole is a stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor and has fewer adverse effects than ethanol. Either way the mechanism is the same: block the enzyme so the toxic metabolites are never formed.",
 quote:"So in the old days, you would go to the emergency room and you would see in rooms in the ERs a bottle of Everclear. It's ethanol, right, with a pretty high percent by volume content of ethanol. ... We now have, uh, the medication omeprazole that we'll give to patients. Um, a little bit better control of the dosing.",
 note:"\"omeprazole\" in the transcript is fomepizole (see L16-008).",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: methanol and ethylene glycol examples’; PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Toxic alcohols’; transcript 10/19"},

/* ---------------- Recognizing the poisoning ---------------- */
{id:"L16-010", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"detect", skill:"apply",
 concept:"diagnosis-symptoms-then-blood", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A sedated, barely responsive patient arrives and methanol or ethylene glycol poisoning is suspected. How is the poisoning confirmed?",
 options:[
  {t:"Classic symptoms first, then a blood draw to look for the alcohols", correct:true, why:"Symptoms suggest the exposure; the key indicator is a blood draw in the emergency room that looks for these alcohols in the blood."},
  {t:"The color of the vomit", correct:false, why:"Color was not a diagnostic sign she gave; the antifreeze color is a dye anyway."},
  {t:"A urine pH measurement", correct:false, why:"Urine pH belongs to ion trapping, not diagnosis."},
  {t:"A liver biopsy showing the pattern of injury that points to the alcohol", correct:false, why:"Biopsy finds a pattern of liver injury; it does not identify an alcohol in the blood."},
  {t:"The smell of antifreeze on the breath", correct:false, why:"The sweet smell explains why children and pets swallow antifreeze, but it does not confirm a poisoning. The key indicator is a blood draw in the emergency room that looks for the alcohols."}],
 teach:"A student asked how anyone would know. People come in with classic symptoms: unconscious, or awake but sedated. The key indicator is a blood draw in the emergency room that looks for the alcohols. More generally, the first guess comes from the symptoms, a toxidrome, and the hospital laboratory then verifies the suspected compound if it can test for it; when a patient intended self-harm and will not say what was taken, or is unconscious, it is sometimes a guess.",
 quote:"How would you know that somebody's got this poisoning? All right, people will come into the ER or maybe you found them and they've got kind of classic symptoms, right? ... The, the, the, the key indicator would be you get them into the ER and they do a blood draw, and they look for these alcohols in their blood.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: methanol and ethylene glycol examples’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-011", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"detect", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"er-blood-tests-list", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which of the following were named as compounds so common in overdose that most emergency rooms test the blood for them? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Acetaminophen", correct:true, why:"Acetaminophen is so common in overdose, and detectable in the blood, that most emergency rooms check for it; it was the first one the room named."},
  {t:"Salicylates", correct:true, why:"Salicylates are common in overdose and detectable in the blood, so most emergency rooms check for them."},
  {t:"Iron", correct:true, why:"Iron is common enough in overdose, and measurable in the blood, that most emergency rooms check for it."},
  {t:"Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers", correct:true, why:"Both cardiac drug classes are common in overdose and detectable in the blood, so most emergency rooms check for them."},
  {t:"Opiates", correct:true, why:"Opiates are also common in overdose and checked in the blood; she added them at the end of the list."},
  {t:"Vitamin K", correct:false, why:"Vitamin K is an antidote, not an overdose screen."},
  {t:"Methylene blue", correct:false, why:"Methylene blue is a treatment in the cyanide scheme."}],
 teach:"Some compounds are so common in overdose, and detectable in blood, that most emergency rooms check for them: acetaminophen, salicylates, iron, beta blockers, calcium channel blockers and opiates, plus a few others. The symptoms give the first guess; the blood test verifies it when the laboratory can run it.",
 quote:"Can you think of any other compounds that it's likely emergency rooms would test a person's blood for to see if they've been exposed to them? Acetaminophen, OK, acetaminophen. Salicylates Iron Beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, and there's a few others, but those are some of the big ones that any patient coming into the ER, oh, opiates as well",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: methanol and ethylene glycol examples’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-012", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"detect", skill:"term",
 concept:"toxidrome-definition", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is a toxidrome?",
 options:[
  {t:"A group of symptoms that together suggest exposure to a particular compound", correct:true, why:"Her words: a group of symptoms that make you say this person has this, this and this, I wonder if they have been exposed to this compound."},
  {t:"A blood test that confirms which poison was taken", correct:false, why:"The blood draw verifies; the toxidrome is the clinical picture that prompts it."},
  {t:"The antidote dose per kilogram of body weight", correct:false, why:"A toxidrome describes the patient, not the treatment: it is the group of symptoms seen together that prompts the first guess about which compound was taken."},
  {t:"The time window within which charcoal still works", correct:false, why:"That is a treatment window, not a symptom pattern."},
  {t:"An evidence-based consensus guideline for the out-of-hospital management of an overdose", correct:false, why:"Those are the AAPCC documents."}],
 teach:"A toxidrome is a group of symptoms that go together and point toward a class of exposure; it is the basis of the first guess before the laboratory verifies the compound. The Goodman and Gilman table on the toxidromes slide lists sympathomimetic, anticholinergic, cholinergic, opioid, sedative-hypnotic, salicylate and calcium channel blocker patterns.",
 quote:"Usually you'll see a group of systems of symptoms that we call a toxic toxodrome that we'll get to at the end of this lecture, kind of a group of symptoms that are like, oh hey, look, this person has this, this and this. I wonder if they've been exposed to this compound.",
 note:"The transcript renders toxidrome as \"toxic toxodrome\". She said she would get to the table at the end of the lecture but the session ended with the guideline discussion; the table itself was not walked through on 10/19.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxidromes’ (Goodman & Gilman Table 4-7); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-013", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"detect", skill:"recall", type:"match", lowYield:true,
 concept:"toxidrome-table", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"Match each toxidrome in the Goodman and Gilman table with its example drugs and distinguishing signs.",
 left:["Sympathomimetic", "Anticholinergic", "Cholinergic", "Opioid", "Salicylate"],
 right:["Cocaine, amphetamine: agitation, tremor, diaphoresis", "Diphenhydramine, atropine: delirium, ileus, flushing", "Organophosphates: somnolence or coma, SLUDGE, fasciculation", "Heroin, oxycodone: somnolence or coma", "Aspirin: confusion, diaphoresis, vomiting"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Sympathomimetic", r:"Cocaine, amphetamine: agitation, tremor, diaphoresis", why:"Table row: sympathomimetic, cocaine and amphetamine, agitation, tremor and diaphoresis."},
  {l:"Anticholinergic", r:"Diphenhydramine, atropine: delirium, ileus, flushing", why:"Table row: anticholinergic, diphenhydramine and atropine, delirium, ileus and flushing."},
  {l:"Cholinergic", r:"Organophosphates: somnolence or coma, SLUDGE, fasciculation", why:"SLUDGE is the table's footnote for the muscarinic effects: salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, gastric cramping and emesis."},
  {l:"Opioid", r:"Heroin, oxycodone: somnolence or coma", why:"Table row: opioid, heroin and oxycodone, somnolence and coma."},
  {l:"Salicylate", r:"Aspirin: confusion, diaphoresis, vomiting", why:"Table row: salicylate, aspirin, confusion, diaphoresis and vomiting."}],
 teach:"The toxidromes slide reproduces Goodman and Gilman's Table 4-7, Common Toxidromes, with columns for drug class, examples, mental status, heart rate, blood pressure, respiratory rate, temperature, pupil size and other signs. The rows are sympathomimetic (cocaine, amphetamine), anticholinergic (diphenhydramine, atropine), cholinergic (organophosphates, with SLUDGE and fasciculation), opioid (heroin, oxycodone), sedative-hypnotic (benzodiazepines, barbiturates), salicylate (aspirin) and calcium channel blocker (verapamil).",
 quote:"symptoms that we call a toxic toxodrome that we'll get to at the end of this lecture",
 note:"Low yield: the table slide was not reached on 10/19; she defined a toxidrome in passing and said the table would come at the end of the lecture, but the session moved to the guideline discussion and ended early. The vital-sign arrows of the table did not survive the text export and are not tested. The objectives slide lists \"Describe the toxidromes discussed in class\".",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxidromes’ (Goodman & Gilman Table 4-7); transcript 10/19"},

/* ---------------- Enhance metabolism ---------------- */
{id:"L16-014", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cofactors-not-induction", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Enzyme inducers exist, yet a poisoning is never treated by inducing the detoxifying enzymes. Why, and what is done instead?",
 options:[
  {t:"Induction takes days; instead, cofactors for the reaction are given", correct:true, why:"The expression of metabolizing enzymes cannot be induced fast enough to help a poisoned patient; giving more of the cofactors the reaction needs can speed it up now."},
  {t:"Inducers are all hepatotoxic; instead, the liver is bypassed with dialysis", correct:false, why:"Toxicity of inducers was not the reason; time was."},
  {t:"Induction only works for phase 2 enzymes; instead, phase 1 is inhibited", correct:false, why:"Phase 1 and phase 2 do not enter into it; the obstacle is time. Induction takes a few days, so instead the existing reactions are sped up by giving their cofactors."},
  {t:"Inducers would also speed bioactivation; instead, the poison is chelated", correct:false, why:"Chelation is for metals; the reason induction fails is speed."},
  {t:"No inducers exist for human enzymes; instead, antibodies are given", correct:false, why:"Inducers do exist, as the class knew; they are just too slow."}],
 teach:"Enhancing metabolism means getting the body to detoxify and clear the compound faster, as long as that metabolism does not make another toxic metabolite. Inducing the enzymes would be ideal but takes a few days, far too slow for a poisoning. What can be done is to supply cofactors for the metabolic reactions so they run faster. Her two examples are cyanide and acetaminophen.",
 quote:"It'd be great if we could induce the expression of enzymes involved in this metabolism, but that is really not possible in a poisoning situation. ... It takes a few days, so we can't treat our patient fast enough with that. Um, so, usually, in the world of a poisoning. What we can do is try to enhance the metabolism by giving cofactors for those metabolizing reactions, OK.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Enhance Metabolism’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-015", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cyanide-target-cco", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is the target of cyanide, and what happens to the poisoned cell?",
 options:[
  {t:"Cytochrome c oxidase; ATP production stops", correct:true, why:"Cyanide binds the enzyme of the electron transport chain and it can do no more work; the person suffocates at the cellular level."},
  {t:"Hemoglobin; oxygen cannot be carried", correct:false, why:"Hemoglobin is used in the treatment, as methemoglobin; it is not cyanide's target."},
  {t:"Acetylcholinesterase; acetylcholine builds up", correct:false, why:"That is the organophosphate target."},
  {t:"Alcohol dehydrogenase; formic acid accumulates", correct:false, why:"That is the methanol story."},
  {t:"Epoxide reductase; vitamin K is not regenerated", correct:false, why:"That is warfarin's target."}],
 teach:"Cytochrome c oxidase is an enzyme of the electron transport chain. Cyanide binds to the flat part of its structure where oxygen would be and inhibits it, so ATP production stops and the patient suffocates even though they are breathing. Cyanide will kill, so the treatment has to get it off the enzyme and then out of the body.",
 quote:"Cytochrome C oxidase is its target. Anybody know what cytochrome cytochrome C oxidase does? Think back to biochemistry. Helps with our electron transport chain, right? ... Cyanide coming in and binding to this enzyme then inhibits its ability to do any work. So, basically, when somebody is exposed to cyanide, you basically suffocate because you're not doing any more of your ATP production, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Cyanide example’ (Golan figure); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-016", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"figure",
 concept:"amyl-nitrite-methemoglobin", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the cyanide treatment figure, what does amyl nitrite do, and why is that acceptable?",
 options:[
  {t:"Oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin, which can take cyanide off the enzyme", correct:true, why:"Changing the charge of the iron makes methemoglobin; it carries no oxygen, but it pulls cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase, which is the point."},
  {t:"Reduces methemoglobin back to oxygen-carrying hemoglobin at the end of treatment", correct:false, why:"That is methylene blue's job."},
  {t:"Supplies the thiosulfate that cyanide is finally bound to", correct:false, why:"Sodium thiosulfate is given separately, as the sulfur donor for the body's rhodanese; amyl nitrite's job is to make methemoglobin."},
  {t:"Binds cyanide directly as a chelator in the plasma", correct:false, why:"Amyl nitrite acts on hemoglobin, not on cyanide."},
  {t:"Regenerates glutathione so that cyanide can be conjugated", correct:false, why:"Glutathione belongs to the acetaminophen scheme."}],
 teach:"The first step is to oxidize hemoglobin with amyl nitrite, changing the charge on its iron, to make methemoglobin. Methemoglobin does not carry oxygen, so it is not good for the patient, but it is created on purpose: its structure resembles the flat part of cytochrome c oxidase, and it pulls cyanide off the enzyme onto itself, forming cyanmethemoglobin and regenerating the enzyme so cellular respiration can continue.",
 quote:"We're gonna oxidize it using this amyl nitrite. To methemoglobin, OK? By changing the iron charge in hemoglobin by oxidizing it using amyl nitrite, you end up with methemoglobin. OK? Is methemoglobin helpful to our bodies? Will it carry oxygen? No, OK, we're still gonna create it in our patients though, because we're trying to save them from their cyanide poisoning, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Cyanide example’ (Golan figure); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-017", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"apply",
 concept:"rhodanese-enhance-metabolism-step", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Three agents are used to treat cyanide poisoning. Which one is the enhance-metabolism step, the cofactor that helps clear the cyanide?",
 options:[
  {t:"Thiosulfate feeding the body's rhodanese", correct:true, why:"Her answer: the rhodanese step. Rhodanese, an enzyme the body already has, moves cyanide onto sulfur from thiosulfate, and the product is renally eliminated; giving sodium thiosulfate supplies the sulfur so the enzyme clears more cyanide."},
  {t:"Amyl nitrite, which makes methemoglobin", correct:false, why:"Amyl nitrite sets up the removal of cyanide from the enzyme; it is not the clearance step."},
  {t:"Methylene blue, which regenerates hemoglobin", correct:false, why:"Methylene blue repairs the methemoglobin the treatment created."},
  {t:"Methemoglobin pulling cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase", correct:false, why:"That step removes the poison from the active site; it does not clear cyanide from the body."},
  {t:"A hemoglobin transfusion to replace the methemoglobin", correct:false, why:"The scheme does not replace methemoglobin; it converts it back: methylene blue, a reducing agent, regenerates hemoglobin. That repairs the blood but does not clear cyanide; the clearing step is rhodanese with thiosulfate."}],
 teach:"Cyanide bound to methemoglobin is less damaging than cyanide on cytochrome c oxidase, but it is still in the body. Rhodanese, an enzyme the body already has, moves the cyanide onto sulfur from thiosulfate, forming thiocyanate, which is renally eliminated. That pathway is capacity limited, so sodium thiosulfate is given to supply the sulfur and speed it up. Supplying what a detoxifying enzyme needs, instead of waiting days for induction, is what makes this the enhance-metabolism step.",
 quote:"The one that is considered um. The enhancing the metabolism is which of the three? Is it the amyl nitrate to reduce hemoglobin to methemoglobin? Is it giving the rhodonase to help thiosulfate bind the cyanide, or is it the methylene blue to regenerate hemoglobin from methemoglobin? Which is the one that I said was the co-factor helping us to clear. Yeah, the roadnas. This is the part of the scheme that is really considered to be enhancing the metabolism and elimination.",
 note:"She said \"giving them this cofactor called rodonase\" and \"thiosulfate is freely in the body\". Read in context, rhodanese is the enzyme this step needs and is already in the body, and sodium thiosulfate is the agent given, as in the assigned Marraffa article: \"Rhodanese, an endogenous enzyme, then facilitates the formation of thiocyanate\" (p. 203) and \"Thiosulfate enhances the activity of rhodanese by donating a sulfur group\" (p. 204). Her exam option \"giving the rhodonase to help thiosulfate bind the cyanide\" names this same step. The transcript renders rhodanese as \"rodonase\", \"rhodonase\", \"roadnas\" and \"Rodinase\". She said the product that is renally eliminated is \"the thiosulfate with a cyanide bound to it\". The cyanide figure did not survive the deck text export; the figure is from Golan's pharmacology textbook.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Cyanide example’ (Golan figure); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-018", dupOf:"L16-017", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"figure", type:"match",
 concept:"cyanide-scheme-steps", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Match each agent in the cyanide treatment scheme with what it does.",
 left:["Amyl nitrite", "Methemoglobin", "Rhodanese", "Methylene blue"],
 right:["Oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin", "Pulls cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase onto itself", "Helps thiosulfate take the cyanide for renal elimination", "Reduces methemoglobin back to hemoglobin"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Amyl nitrite", r:"Oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin", why:"Changes the iron charge of hemoglobin."},
  {l:"Methemoglobin", r:"Pulls cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase onto itself", why:"Forms cyanmethemoglobin and frees the enzyme; this is also the removal-from-the-active-site step."},
  {l:"Rhodanese", r:"Helps thiosulfate take the cyanide for renal elimination", why:"The body's own enzyme in the enhance-metabolism step; given sodium thiosulfate supplies its sulfur."},
  {l:"Methylene blue", r:"Reduces methemoglobin back to hemoglobin", why:"A reducing agent; the patient's blood could not carry oxygen otherwise."}],
 teach:"The scheme runs in order. Amyl nitrite oxidizes hemoglobin to methemoglobin. Methemoglobin pulls cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase, regenerating the enzyme. Rhodanese helps thiosulfate take the cyanide off methemoglobin, and that product (thiocyanate on her figure; she called it \"the thiosulfate bound to cyanide\") leaves in the urine. The patient is still left with methemoglobin that cannot carry oxygen, so methylene blue, a reducing agent, converts it back to hemoglobin. Only the rhodanese step is the enhance-metabolism example; the methemoglobin step is the remove-from-the-active-site example.",
 quote:"And methemoglobin is able to pull cyanide off of cytochrome C oxidase. ... amyl nitrate makes the methemoglobin, methylene reconverts methemoglobin back to hemoglobin, OK? And then the rodonase is what's really helping us to enhance the, the metabolism and then elimination of the cyanide.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Cyanide example’ (Golan figure); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-019", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"figure",
 concept:"nac-glutathione-ingredients", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the acetaminophen scheme, what is N-acetylcysteine (NAC) doing?",
 options:[
  {t:"Supplying ingredients to make more glutathione", correct:true, why:"The body makes and stores glutathione all the time; NAC provides some of the ingredients needed to make more, so more NAPQI gets conjugated and eliminated."},
  {t:"Neutralizing the free radical directly", correct:false, why:"A student's answer, and a good thought, but no: the free radical NAPQI is the problem, and NAC works through glutathione."},
  {t:"Inhibiting the enzyme that bioactivates acetaminophen to NAPQI", correct:false, why:"That would be inhibiting bioactivation; NAC enhances the clearance of what has already formed."},
  {t:"Chelating NAPQI in the plasma", correct:false, why:"Chelators bind metals. NAPQI is cleared by conjugation with glutathione, and NAC works by supplying ingredients to make more glutathione."},
  {t:"Inducing the glutathione S-transferases", correct:false, why:"Induction is too slow for a poisoning; NAC supplies a cofactor."}],
 teach:"Acetaminophen is metabolized to NAPQI, a free radical that damages the liver. Glutathione, with the GST enzymes, conjugates NAPQI so it can be eliminated. The body makes glutathione continuously through a complicated metabolic scheme and keeps stores of it, but an overdose uses them up. NAC, given as a drug, provides ingredients for making more glutathione, so it is the cofactor that enhances the metabolism and elimination of NAPQI.",
 quote:"No, it's not neutralizing free radicals. Good, good point though, because we do have free radicals being the problem here, right? ... what's shown on this slide is anastylcysteine, we give it as a drug. And it's giving ingredients to the system to create more glutathione.",
 note:"The transcript renders NAPQI as \"NAQI\" and \"nap QI\", and N-acetylcysteine as \"acylcysteine\", \"anastylcysteine\", \"NAStylcysteine\". She once misspoke \"nap QI provides ingredients for making more glutathione\" (meaning NAC).",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Acetaminophen Example’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-020", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"recall",
 concept:"glutathione-gst-conjugates-napqi", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which compound in the body conjugates NAPQI, the toxic metabolite of acetaminophen, and with which enzyme family?",
 options:[
  {t:"Glutathione, with the glutathione S-transferases (GST)", correct:true, why:"Glutathione grabs NAPQI with the help of the GST enzymes; glutathione is one of the body's powerhouse reducing agents."},
  {t:"Thiosulfate, with rhodanese", correct:false, why:"That pair belongs to cyanide."},
  {t:"Vitamin K, with the vitamin K dependent carboxylase of the liver", correct:false, why:"That pair belongs to coagulation factors and warfarin."},
  {t:"Methemoglobin, made by amyl nitrite", correct:false, why:"Methemoglobin is made on purpose in the cyanide scheme to pull cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase; it does not conjugate NAPQI. That job belongs to glutathione, with the GST enzymes."},
  {t:"Sulfate, with alcohol dehydrogenase", correct:false, why:"Alcohol dehydrogenase bioactivates methanol and ethylene glycol."}],
 teach:"NAPQI is the free radical acetaminophen is metabolized to, and it damages the liver. Glutathione conjugates it with the help of the GST enzymes. Because the body's glutathione is made continuously and can run out in an overdose, the treatment supplies NAC to make more.",
 quote:"What is in our body that can help us clear NAQI? What, what compound grabs NAQI, conjugates it? Glutathione, with the help of the GST enzymes, yes, you end up conjugating NAQI with glutathione.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Acetaminophen Example’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-021", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"apply",
 concept:"nac-iv-smell", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"N-acetylcysteine (NAC) can be given orally or intravenously. Why is it usually given intravenously in the United States, and what caution goes with that?",
 options:[
  {t:"It smells like rotten eggs; give only the IV formulation by IV", correct:true, why:"The sulfur smell and oily clear liquid are hard to swallow; where a hospital stocks both protocols, the IV formulation, not the oral one, goes in the vein."},
  {t:"It is destroyed by stomach acid; the oral form must be enteric-coated", correct:false, why:"Oral NAC works and can be given. The IV route is preferred because NAC smells of sulfur, like rotten eggs, and is oily, so it is hard to get a patient to swallow."},
  {t:"It is too oily to absorb; the oral form is only for children", correct:false, why:"Oral NAC can be given; the problem is getting anyone to swallow it."},
  {t:"It stains the patient blue; gloves must be worn", correct:false, why:"Methylene blue stains; NAC stinks."},
  {t:"It is a controlled substance; the pharmacy must log each vial", correct:false, why:"The pharmacy issue with NAC is its sulfur smell, known to anyone who has seen a glass bottle break; the clinical caution is formulation: only the IV product goes into a vein."}],
 teach:"NAC comes in glass bottles and smells of sulfur, like rotten eggs; everyone in a hospital pharmacy knows when a bottle has shattered. Because it is hard to get a patient to swallow an oily, stinking liquid, it is usually given by IV in the United States. The caution: you cannot give an oral product intravenously, so where both protocols exist, make sure the IV formulation is the one given IV.",
 quote:"It stinks, smells like rotten eggs. So, it's got a sulfur smell. ... So, in the United States, we tend to give it by IV because it's pretty hard to get somebody to swallow something that smells like rotten eggs, and it's kind of oily and, it's a clear liquid, it's kind of disgusting. ... If, if they have an oral formulation, make sure you are giving the IV formulation if you're giving it IV, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Acetaminophen Example’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-022", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"metab", skill:"tell",
 concept:"examples-by-toxicokinetic-method", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which two poisonings are the examples of enhancing metabolism (detoxification) by giving a cofactor?",
 options:[
  {t:"Cyanide and acetaminophen", correct:true, why:"Sodium thiosulfate for cyanide (sulfur for the body's rhodanese) and NAC for acetaminophen (ingredients for glutathione) are the two examples."},
  {t:"Methanol and ethylene glycol", correct:false, why:"Those are the inhibit-bioactivation examples."},
  {t:"Salicylates and phenobarbital", correct:false, why:"Those are the ion-trapping examples for enhancing renal elimination."},
  {t:"Organophosphates and belladonna alkaloids", correct:false, why:"Those are pharmacological examples: atropine, pralidoxime, physostigmine."},
  {t:"Warfarin and digoxin", correct:false, why:"Neither speeds a detoxifying reaction. Vitamin K bypasses the pathway warfarin blocks, a pharmacological treatment, and digoxin immune Fab is an antibody that binds digoxin, an inactivation treatment."}],
 teach:"Enhancing metabolism means speeding up a detoxifying reaction the body already runs. Enzyme induction takes days, too slow for a poisoning, so a cofactor is supplied instead. For cyanide, sodium thiosulfate supplies sulfur so the body's rhodanese can convert cyanide to thiocyanate for renal elimination. For acetaminophen, NAC provides ingredients for glutathione, which conjugates NAPQI. Methanol and ethylene glycol are the opposite case: there the enzyme is blocked so a toxic metabolite is never made.",
 quote:"And the two examples that I have that we're going to talk about are treating cyanide poisonings and treating acetaminophen poisonings.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Enhance Metabolism’; transcript 10/19"},

/* ---------------- Enhance renal elimination ---------------- */
{id:"L16-023", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"renal", skill:"apply",
 concept:"reabsorption-continues-poisoning", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A poison is filtered at the glomerulus into the forming urine, yet the poisoning continues. Why?",
 options:[
  {t:"It is reabsorbed from the forming urine back into the circulation", correct:true, why:"Many compounds are filtered and then reabsorbed, which continues the poisoning; preventing that reabsorption is the goal of ion trapping."},
  {t:"The glomerulus filters only the metabolites and never the parent compound", correct:false, why:"The parent compound is filtered; the problem comes afterward."},
  {t:"Urine is not formed during a poisoning", correct:false, why:"Urine is formed; what matters is what is reabsorbed from it."},
  {t:"The kidney bioactivates the poison in the tubule", correct:false, why:"Nothing is made from the poison in the nephron; the problem is reabsorption. The filtered poison passes from the forming urine back into the blood, so the poisoning continues."},
  {t:"Filtered poison binds to charcoal in the bladder", correct:false, why:"Charcoal acts in the gut."}],
 teach:"Enhancing renal elimination is a common way to treat acute poisonings when it can be done. Compounds get filtered at the glomerulus into the renal lumen, but many are then reabsorbed from the forming urine into the systemic circulation, which keeps the poisoning going. The treatment prevents that reabsorption by ion trapping.",
 quote:"Things that people are exposed to, they get filtered at the glomerulus, get into the urine that's being formed, but then will get reabsorbed. So you continue the poisoning by the reabsorption of that compound from the forming urine into the systemic circulation.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Enhance Renal Elimination’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-024", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"renal", skill:"apply",
 concept:"bicarbonate-ion-trapping-acids", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient has overdosed on salicylates or phenobarbital, both mildly acidic. What is given to enhance renal elimination, and what does it do to the urine?",
 options:[
  {t:"IV sodium bicarbonate, to make the urine more basic", correct:true, why:"The bicarbonate raises the urine pH, the weak acid becomes charged, and a charged molecule cannot be reabsorbed: ion trapping."},
  {t:"An IV acidifying agent, to make the urine more acidic", correct:false, why:"Acidifying is dangerous and is not done for weak acids."},
  {t:"Cranberry juice, to acidify the urine", correct:false, why:"Cranberry juice acidifies a little, not enough and not fast enough, and acid is the wrong direction for an acidic poison."},
  {t:"Activated charcoal, to bind the drug in the tubule", correct:false, why:"Charcoal works in the gut, not the nephron."},
  {t:"Hemodialysis, to remove it from the plasma", correct:false, why:"Dialysis is extracorporeal, reserved for severe poisonings."}],
 teach:"At normal urine pH a weakly acidic poison such as a salicylate or phenobarbital is uncharged and gets reabsorbed. Hanging a bag of IV sodium bicarbonate makes the urine more basic; the acid then carries a charge, cannot be reabsorbed, stays in the urine and is excreted. The slide calls it very effective for salicylates and phenobarbital. No Henderson-Hasselbalch exercises: she said to take her word that bicarbonate raises the pH enough.",
 quote:"So if we have a person that's overdosed on salicylates on some of our barbiturates, they are mildly acidic. And get filtered at the glomerulus. They're in the urine, and at normal urine pH, they are not charged, OK? But if we will try to make the urine more basic by giving the patient sodium bicarb by IV, the sodium bicarb goes into the urine and will charge that acid, and it can't be reabsorbed when it's charged. OK, so we call that ion trapping",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Enhance Renal Elimination’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-025", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"renal", skill:"apply",
 concept:"charged-not-reabsorbed", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"After sodium bicarbonate raises the urine pH, what charge does a mildly acidic poison carry, and why does that stop reabsorption?",
 options:[
  {t:"Negative; the reabsorption transporters move uncharged molecules", correct:true, why:"Her answer: negative charge. The active transporters that pull solutes from urine back into blood need their substrates, for the most part, uncharged."},
  {t:"Positive; positive ions bind to the tubule wall", correct:false, why:"An acid in basic urine loses a proton and goes negative."},
  {t:"No charge; uncharged molecules are too large for the transporters to reabsorb", correct:false, why:"The uncharged form is the one that is reabsorbed."},
  {t:"Negative; it then binds charcoal in the urine", correct:false, why:"No charcoal in the nephron; the transporters are the reason."},
  {t:"Positive; the glomerulus will not filter it again", correct:false, why:"An acid in basic urine carries a negative charge, not a positive one, and filtration is unchanged. What changes is reabsorption: the transporters mostly move uncharged molecules, so the charged poison stays in the urine."}],
 teach:"Raising the pH turns the slightly acidic poison into its ionized state with a negative charge. The transporters that reabsorb solutes from urine into blood are active transport processes that mostly handle uncharged molecules, so the charged poison stays in the urine and is passed out. That is what ion trapping means.",
 quote:"What kind of charge will it have if you are iron trapping your your mildly acidic poison. Negative charge, right? So it's not going to be reabsorbed when it has that charge on it, because a lot of our transporters that do reabsorption of solutes from the urine back into the blood, uh, are doing it. It's they're active transport processes, and those things have to be, um, for the most part uncharged, OK.",
 note:"The transcript renders ion trapping as \"iron trapping\" in places.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Enhance Renal Elimination’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-026", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"renal", skill:"apply",
 concept:"no-trapping-for-bases", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why is ion trapping not used for poisons that are weakly basic?",
 options:[
  {t:"Acidifying a patient's blood to acidify the urine is dangerous", correct:true, why:"The opposite direction would require acidifying the person; cranberry juice does not lower the pH enough or fast enough, and anything stronger is risky."},
  {t:"Weak bases are never filtered at the glomerulus", correct:false, why:"They are filtered; the problem is how to trap them."},
  {t:"Bicarbonate cannot be given to a patient who took a base", correct:false, why:"Bicarbonate is not forbidden; it simply makes the urine more basic, which is what charges an acidic poison. Trapping a base would require acidifying the urine, which means acidifying the patient's blood, and that is dangerous."},
  {t:"Basic poisons have no transporter for reabsorption", correct:false, why:"They are reabsorbed like other uncharged solutes."},
  {t:"Cranberry juice acidifies the urine fast enough, so no drug is needed", correct:false, why:"Cranberry juice is too weak and too slow for a poisoning."}],
 teach:"Ion trapping is safe and quite successful for slightly acidic poisons: make the urine more basic. The reverse, trapping a weakly basic poison by acidifying the urine, would mean acidifying the patient's blood, which is dangerous. Cranberry juice, used for mild urinary tract infections, acidifies a little but not to a low enough pH and not fast enough to treat a poisoning.",
 quote:"we don't tend to do this for poisons that are weakly basic, because acidifying a person's blood to try to get their urine more acidic is kind of dangerous, OK? You could try maybe getting them to drink cranberry juice, right? ... but you really don't get a lot of acidification of the urine by drinking cranberry juice, not high enough or not, not low enough pH and not fast enough for treating somebody that has ingested a basic type of poison, OK",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Enhance Renal Elimination’; transcript 10/19"},

/* ---------------- Extracorporeal ---------------- */
{id:"L16-027", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"extra", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"extracorporeal-methods", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following are extracorporeal (out of the body) methods of enhancing elimination? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Hemodialysis", correct:true, why:"An out-of-the-body method: the blood is artificially cleared of the toxin outside the patient. Whether it works depends on the poison's chemistry, since dialysis does not grab everything."},
  {t:"Hemofiltration", correct:true, why:"An out-of-the-body method: the blood is artificially filtered clear of the toxin outside the patient."},
  {t:"Hemoperfusion", correct:true, why:"An out-of-the-body method: the blood is artificially cleared of the toxin outside the patient, alongside hemodialysis and hemofiltration."},
  {t:"Plasma exchange or exchange transfusion", correct:true, why:"For people who are really bad; a really bad poisoning."},
  {t:"Whole bowel irrigation", correct:false, why:"That prevents absorption in the gut; nothing leaves the body for processing."},
  {t:"Ion trapping with sodium bicarbonate", correct:false, why:"That uses the patient's own kidneys."}],
 teach:"Extracorporeal means out of the body: the blood is artificially cleared of toxin by hemodialysis, hemofiltration or hemoperfusion, or, for the worst cases, by plasma exchange or exchange transfusion. Unlike ion trapping, which uses the patient's own kidneys, or whole bowel irrigation, which acts in the gut, these methods act on toxin already in the blood. Whether they work depends on the poison's chemistry, and they are kept for very severe poisonings where other treatments are failing.",
 quote:"What this means is you're going to try to artificially clear the blood of toxins, so maybe using hemodialysis, hemofiltration, or hemo perfusion. Um, you might try to do plasma exchange or whole, whole exchange transfusions for people that are really bad, but this is like a really, really bad poisoning, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: Extracorporeal enhancement of elimination’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-028", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"extra", skill:"apply",
 concept:"extracorporeal-when-and-whether", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What decides whether hemodialysis, hemofiltration or hemoperfusion will work for a given poisoning, and when are they used?",
 options:[
  {t:"The chemistry of the poison; for very severe poisonings", correct:true, why:"Dialysis does not grab everything and filtration does not block everything; these methods are for severe cases where other things are not working."},
  {t:"The patient's age; for every ingestion", correct:false, why:"Age does not decide it; the poison's chemistry does, since dialysis does not grab everything. And these methods are not routine: they are kept for very severe poisonings when other treatments are failing."},
  {t:"The urine pH; for weak acids only", correct:false, why:"Urine pH is the ion-trapping story."},
  {t:"The formulation of the product; for extended-release tablets only", correct:false, why:"Formulation decides the guideline time windows, not dialysis."},
  {t:"The time elapsed after ingestion; within 2 hours", correct:false, why:"That is the charcoal window."}],
 teach:"Whether an out-of-the-body method works depends on the chemistry of what the person took; dialysis does not grab everything. The slide adds that it only works for toxins with a small volume of distribution, so that clearing the blood does not leave a large inaccessible reservoir in the tissues (the large-Vd problem from 10/14). These methods are used for very severe poisonings when other treatments are failing: the patient is rolled down to dialysis.",
 quote:"the chemistry of whatever a person's been poisoned with is going to determine whether or not these extracorporeal or out of the body methods are going to work, right? Dialysis doesn't grab everything. ... Usually they are for very, very severe poisonings where other things are not working.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment: Extracorporeal enhancement of elimination’; transcript 10/19"},

/* ---------------- Inactivation: principles and chelators ---------------- */
{id:"L16-029", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"inact", skill:"apply",
 concept:"inactivation-after-absorption", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"At what point in a poisoning does inactivation of the poison, an antidote in the official sense, apply?",
 options:[
  {t:"Only once the poison has been absorbed into the systemic circulation", correct:true, why:"Preventing absorption comes first; if that fails and the compound is in the circulation, an inactivator can bind it there."},
  {t:"Only while the poison is still in the stomach", correct:false, why:"That is the window for charcoal and the other absorption-prevention methods."},
  {t:"Only after the poison has been bioactivated", correct:false, why:"Inactivators bind the toxin itself."},
  {t:"Only after hemodialysis and the other extracorporeal methods have failed", correct:false, why:"Dialysis is the last resort for severe cases; inactivation does not wait for it."},
  {t:"Before any exposure, as prophylaxis", correct:false, why:"An inactivator binds a toxin that is already in the circulation; with no poison in the blood there is nothing for it to bind."}],
 teach:"The biggest thing in the toxicokinetic approach is to prevent absorption. When that fails, the poison is in the circulation; the other kinetic methods apply, and for some compounds there is what is officially called an antidote, an inactivator that binds the poison in the blood so it cannot reach its target and the complex is cleared. Inactivators come in two groups: heavy metal chelators, and antivenoms and antibodies.",
 quote:"this will work only once the compound unfortunately has been absorbed into the systemic circulation, right? ... And then for some compounds, you might have what we officially call an antidote, which is what we're talking about now, OK? Those would be things that actually inactivate a poison.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-030", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"inact", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"inactivator-requirements", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following are requirements of a good inactivator of a poison? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"It binds the toxin so the toxin cannot interact with its target", correct:true, why:"The mechanism of inactivation: bind the whole poison, not just a part that leaves the active part free."},
  {t:"The toxin-inactivator complex is cleared from the body", correct:true, why:"Binding stops the toxin from reaching its target; the complex then has to be cleared so that the toxin actually leaves the body."},
  {t:"The complex has no toxicity of its own", correct:true, why:"The slide says low toxicity; she changed it to no toxicity: the bound poison must not be poisonous anymore."},
  {t:"It has high affinity for the toxin", correct:true, why:"So that massive doses of inactivator, with their own side effects, are not needed."},
  {t:"It has low affinity, so that large doses can be given safely", correct:false, why:"The reverse: high affinity means a small dose suffices."},
  {t:"It binds only part of the toxin, leaving its active site free", correct:false, why:"Her drawing: an inactivator that grabs only a little bit leaves the active part finding the target."}],
 teach:"An inactivator binds the toxin and prevents it from interacting with its target; the toxin-inactivator complex must then be cleared, must have no activity of its own, and the inactivator must have high affinity for the toxin so that huge doses, which could cause side effects, are unnecessary. She drew a poison grabbed whole by a good inactivator versus one grabbed at one end, with the rest still able to touch the target.",
 quote:"they will bind to the toxin and prevent it from interacting with its target, OK? Um, That toxin inactivator complex then has to get cleared from the body. And that complex of toxin and inactivator has to not have its own activity, right? ... so the complex must have, let's change that from low to no toxicity here at this bottom bullet, OK. And then Uh, a good inactivator needs to have really good affinity for the toxin.",
 note:"Slide: \"Complex must have low toxicity\". Transcript: she changed it to \"no toxicity\". Keyed on her wording.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-031", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"inact", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"chelator-properties", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which statements describe heavy metal chelators? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Small molecules with a nucleophilic electron that forms a metal-ligand complex", correct:true, why:"The outer-shell electron binds the more positive metal, tightly."},
  {t:"Usually multidentate", correct:true, why:"Several binding points on one metal atom."},
  {t:"Higher affinity for the toxic metal than the body's own macromolecules have", correct:true, why:"Otherwise the metal stays on the tissue."},
  {t:"Water soluble, non-toxic and readily cleared", correct:true, why:"The aim is to remove the metal from the patient, so the chelator must dissolve in water, be cleared readily, and do no harm of its own."},
  {t:"Low affinity for endogenous ions such as calcium", correct:true, why:"The ideal chelator leaves the ions the body needs alone; calcium is the most common concern."},
  {t:"Large proteins made by inoculating animals", correct:false, why:"That is how polyclonal antivenoms are made."},
  {t:"High affinity for calcium and magnesium", correct:false, why:"That is exactly what a chelator must not have; some enzymes carry magnesium and the body needs its calcium."}],
 teach:"Chelators bind lead, mercury, cadmium, iron and copper. They are small molecules with a nucleophilic electron donor that forms a metal-ligand complex, usually multidentate. They must bind the poisoning metal more tightly than the body's macromolecules do, must not grab the positive ions the body needs such as magnesium in enzymes or calcium, and must be water soluble, non-toxic and readily cleared.",
 quote:"the chelators are actually small molecules, and they have a nucleophilic electron. ... It has a multidentate structure, usually is what our chelators have ... Again, the chelating agent must have a higher affinity for binding the toxic metal than macromolecules naturally in your body. ... Needs to be water soluble and non-toxic and readily cleared, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons: Heavy Metal Chelators’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-032", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"inact", skill:"figure",
 concept:"multidentate-meaning", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the figure of chelator structures, one molecule looks like a spider with its legs on a single copper atom. What does multidentate mean?",
 options:[
  {t:"One chelator molecule binds a single metal atom at several points", correct:true, why:"The legs of the spider are the several ways one molecule grips one atom of the metal."},
  {t:"Several metal atoms share one binding site on the chelator", correct:false, why:"It is one metal atom held at many points, not many metals at one point."},
  {t:"The chelator has several teeth that cut the metal out of the enzyme", correct:false, why:"Dentate refers to binding points, not cutting."},
  {t:"The chelator binds the metal covalently at one point only", correct:false, why:"That would be monodentate; the figure also shows several molecules of a ligand binding one metal."},
  {t:"The metal is bound by several different chelating drugs given together", correct:false, why:"Combination dosing is not what the figure shows."}],
 teach:"The Golan figure shows two arrangements: several ligand molecules binding one metal atom, and one multidentate molecule binding one metal atom at several points, the spider whose legs hold a copper atom. The structures of dimercaprol, EDTA and penicillamine are shown but do not need to be known; the figure is there to show what multidentate means.",
 quote:"So this one molecule here is the chelator. And this is what we mean by multidentate. It has several ways that it's going to bind one atom of that metal. ... Like that kind of looks like a spider, right? The legs are binding one atom of that copper, OK.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons Structure of chelators’ (Golan figure); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-033", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"inact", skill:"drug", type:"match",
 concept:"chelator-metal-table", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Match each chelating agent with the metal or metals it chelates.",
 left:["Edetate disodium", "Dimercaprol (British anti-Lewisite, BAL)", "Succimer", "Deferoxamine", "Deferasirox", "Penicillamine and trientine"],
 right:["Lead", "Gold, arsenic, lead, mercury", "Lead, cadmium, mercury, arsenic", "Iron", "Iron; orally bioavailable, recently FDA approved", "Copper, usually in Wilson's disease"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Edetate disodium", r:"Lead", why:"Table row: edetate disodium, lead."},
  {l:"Dimercaprol (British anti-Lewisite, BAL)", r:"Gold, arsenic, lead, mercury", why:"Table row: dimercaprol, gold, arsenic, lead, mercury."},
  {l:"Succimer", r:"Lead, cadmium, mercury, arsenic", why:"Table row: succimer, lead, cadmium, mercury, arsenic."},
  {l:"Deferoxamine", r:"Iron", why:"One of the two iron chelators."},
  {l:"Deferasirox", r:"Iron; orally bioavailable, recently FDA approved", why:"The oral iron chelator."},
  {l:"Penicillamine and trientine", r:"Copper, usually in Wilson's disease", why:"Table row: penicillamine and trientine, copper, usually for patients with Wilson's disease."}],
 teach:"The table of the most common chelating agents is to be memorized: agent and the metal it grabs. Edetate disodium for lead; dimercaprol (BAL) for gold, arsenic, lead and mercury; succimer for lead, cadmium, mercury and arsenic; deferoxamine and the oral deferasirox for iron; penicillamine and trientine for copper in Wilson's disease. Side effects are not covered.",
 quote:"This is a table of our most common chelating agents. We don't have a lot out there. And so, you need to know these chelating agents and which ones bind which metals, OK? ... All right, so, please memorize this table, uh, know the chelating agent and know what metal it grabs.",
 note:"In the deck text export the comment \"Taking place of Dimercaprol\" sits between the succimer and deferoxamine rows; it is read as the succimer comment. The transcript renders deferoxamine and deferasirox as \"differoxyzine\" and \"differracerro\"/\"differracerox\".",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons: Example Chelating Agents’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-034", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"inact", skill:"drug",
 concept:"iron-chelators-oral", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Two iron chelators are on the market. Which one can be given orally?",
 options:[
  {t:"Deferasirox", correct:true, why:"When it came on the market it was nice: an oral iron chelator; the slide calls it orally bioavailable and recently FDA approved."},
  {t:"Deferoxamine", correct:false, why:"The older iron chelator, not the oral one."},
  {t:"Dimercaprol", correct:false, why:"BAL chelates gold, arsenic, lead and mercury."},
  {t:"Succimer", correct:false, why:"Succimer chelates lead, cadmium, mercury and arsenic, not iron. The two iron chelators are deferoxamine and deferasirox, and deferasirox is the oral one."},
  {t:"Edetate disodium", correct:false, why:"Edetate disodium is a lead chelator, not an iron one. Of the two iron chelators, deferasirox is the one given orally."}],
 teach:"Iron is an interesting overdose: the body needs it and must absorb it, but it is dangerous in excess, and an overdose blows apart the gut's saturable transport (10/14). Two chelators bind iron, deferoxamine and deferasirox; deferasirox is the oral one.",
 quote:"We have had two iron chelating agents on the market now, differoxyzine and differracerro. Um, the differracerox, when it came on the market, was nice cause it was, um, it's oral. You can give them, give this chelating agent orally, OK, for iron.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons: Example Chelating Agents’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-035", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"inact", skill:"drug",
 concept:"wilsons-copper-chelation", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Patients with Wilson's disease are treated with a chelator. Which metal, and which agents?",
 options:[
  {t:"Copper; penicillamine or trientine", correct:true, why:"Wilson's disease is a genetically based disease with very high copper levels that damage organs; penicillamine or now trientine chelates the copper."},
  {t:"Iron; deferoxamine or deferasirox", correct:false, why:"Those are the iron chelators."},
  {t:"Lead; edetate disodium or succimer", correct:false, why:"Edetate disodium and succimer chelate lead. Wilson's disease is a copper problem, so the chelators are penicillamine or trientine."},
  {t:"Mercury; dimercaprol", correct:false, why:"Dimercaprol is for gold, arsenic, lead and mercury."},
  {t:"Gold; penicillamine", correct:false, why:"Gold is chelated by dimercaprol; penicillamine is the copper agent."}],
 teach:"Copper is not something a person normally ingests in excess, but Wilson's disease, a genetically based condition, produces very high copper levels that damage organs. Penicillamine, or now trientine, is used to chelate the copper out of these patients.",
 quote:"Copper is listed as a metal that we will chelate out of patients that have a disease called Wilson's disease. ... we'll actually use, um, penicilamine or now trentian, Uh, I'm sorry, trientine to try to chelate the copper off of these patients with Wilson's disease.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons: Example Chelating Agents’; transcript 10/19"},

/* ---------------- Antivenoms and antibodies ---------------- */
{id:"L16-036", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"antibody", skill:"recall",
 concept:"polyclonal-antivenom-production", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"How is a polyclonal antivenom made, and what does polyclonal mean?",
 options:[
  {t:"Antibodies collected from the blood of an animal inoculated with small amounts of venom; a mixture, not one clone", correct:true, why:"The animal is not killed; its immune system makes antibodies against the venom, the blood is drawn and the antibodies filtered out: a collection, not a single genetically identical antibody."},
  {t:"One antibody-producing cell line is cloned in culture and purified; a single genetically identical antibody produced in bulk", correct:false, why:"That is a monoclonal antibody, which she said she would explain later."},
  {t:"Antibodies are extracted from the blood of people who survived bites; a mixture from many human donors", correct:false, why:"The source is an inoculated animal, not survivors."},
  {t:"The venom's receptor is synthesized chemically and given as a decoy; not an antibody at all", correct:false, why:"Antivenoms are antibodies against the venom."},
  {t:"The patient is given a small dose of venom to raise their own antibodies; a vaccine", correct:false, why:"The bitten patient has no time to make antibodies; they are given the animal's."}],
 teach:"Antivenoms are antibodies against a venom, and most are still polyclonal. An animal is given small, non-lethal amounts of a snake venom so that its immune system makes antibodies; its blood is drawn and the antibodies filtered out. The product is a collection of different antibodies against the venom, not one genetically identical antibody, which is what polyclonal means. Monoclonals for venoms are being worked on; the antibody antidote in use for a drug is digoxin immune Fab, which is made from sheep antibody fragments.",
 quote:"a polyclonal antibody, we will inoculate an animal. With small amounts of a snake venom. ... The animal's immune system creates antibodies against the venom, and then you're gonna draw their blood, and you can actually basically filter out those antibodies that that animal made against that venom. OK? ... It's a collection of antibodies. They are made to fight that venom, but it's not just one single. Genetically identical antibody, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons: Antivenoms and Antibodies’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-037", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"antibody", skill:"apply",
 concept:"serum-sickness-type-iii", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A snake-bite patient given a polyclonal antivenom develops fever, nausea and malaise. What is happening?",
 options:[
  {t:"A type III hypersensitivity reaction, serum sickness, against the foreign antibodies", correct:true, why:"The body is fighting the venom and now a flood of antibodies made by another animal; the reaction to the polyclonal antivenom is serum sickness."},
  {t:"The antivenom is too specific to cover the subspecies of rattlesnake", correct:false, why:"Symptoms of the venom are a different problem; the fever, nausea and malaise are the reaction to the antivenom."},
  {t:"The antivenom is monoclonal and cannot bind a mixed venom", correct:false, why:"Most antivenoms are polyclonal, and that is the cause of the reaction."},
  {t:"The venom has aged on its target enzyme and the polyclonal antivenom can no longer pull it off", correct:false, why:"Aging is an organophosphate story."},
  {t:"Type I anaphylaxis, which every antivenom causes", correct:false, why:"The reaction she named is type III, serum sickness, and not every patient gets it."}],
 teach:"Polyclonal antivenoms are hard to use. A patient already fighting a snake bite is flooded with antibodies made by some other animal, and the immune system reacts to them: a type III hypersensitivity reaction, also called serum sickness, with fever, nausea and malaise. That danger is why people are working on monoclonal antivenoms. Antivenom supply is also a problem: small rural hospitals do not stock it, and hospitals locate it through a network, probably through the poison centers.",
 quote:"oftentimes you end up with people having a certain kind of hypersensitivity reaction called type 3 hypersensitivity. Did you guys talk about that in your immune immunology class? Remember, that's also called serum sickness. ... you have a fever, nausea, malaise, OK? Because your body's also fighting those polyclonal antibodies that you just gave them",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons: Antivenoms and Antibodies’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-038", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"antibody", skill:"drug",
 concept:"digoxin-immune-fab", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is digoxin immune Fab?",
 options:[
  {t:"An antibody that binds overdosed digoxin and clears it from the blood", correct:true, why:"Her example of an antibody that treats a drug overdose: one form of antibody, made to grab digoxin and clear it. Her table key: digoxin specific antibody fragments bind digoxin, which will be renally eliminated."},
  {t:"A polyclonal antivenom raised in animals against the foxglove plant", correct:false, why:"It is made in sheep, but against digoxin itself, and it is antibody fragments (Fab) that bind the drug, not an antivenom against a plant."},
  {t:"A receptor antagonist that blocks digoxin at its binding site on the heart", correct:false, why:"It inactivates the drug in the blood rather than acting at the receptor."},
  {t:"A chelator for the metal in the digoxin molecule", correct:false, why:"Digoxin is not a metal; chelators do not apply."},
  {t:"A vitamin that bypasses the pathway digoxin inhibits", correct:false, why:"That is the warfarin and vitamin K category."}],
 teach:"Among the inactivators, antivenoms are antibodies against venoms, and one antibody treats a drug overdose: digoxin immune Fab, sheep-derived antibody fragments (ovine Fab) made to grab digoxin and clear it out of the blood. Like any inactivator, it binds the toxin so it cannot reach its target, and the complex is cleared. After Fab is given, total digoxin (free and bound to Fab) is increased, so serum levels cannot be used to calculate more Fab doses.",
 quote:"the antibody that we have is the example I have here is digoxin immune fab. This is an antibody, a monoclonal antibody that's made to treat a digoxin overdose, OK? ... One form of antibody made to grab digoxin that somebody's overdosed on, it grabs it and nicely clears it out of the blood.",
 note:"She called digoxin immune Fab a monoclonal antibody; the FDA label (DigiFab, Digoxin Immune Fab (Ovine)) describes ovine Fab immunoglobulin fragments isolated from the serum of sheep immunized with a digoxin derivative, and the Marraffa article calls it digoxin-specific antibody fragments. The slide lists it only as the example under antivenoms and antibodies.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Inactivation of Poisons: Antivenoms and Antibodies’; PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Digoxin’; transcript 10/19"},

/* ---------------- Pharmacological treatment I ---------------- */
{id:"L16-039", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmrec", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"four-pharmacological-categories", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following are the four categories of pharmacologically based treatment of a poisoning? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"The treatment is a receptor antagonist", correct:true, why:"Blocks the effect of a toxin that acts as an agonist: naloxone at the opioid receptor, flumazenil at the GABA receptor, atropine against excess acetylcholine."},
  {t:"The treatment is a receptor agonist", correct:true, why:"Blocks the effect of a toxin that acts as an antagonist: physostigmine raises acetylcholine against the anticholinergic belladonna alkaloids."},
  {t:"The treatment removes the poison from the active site", correct:true, why:"Pulls the poison off the site it is bound to instead of acting at a receptor: methemoglobin takes cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase and pralidoxime takes the organophosphate off acetylcholinesterase; the slide adds oxygen displacing carbon monoxide from hemoglobin."},
  {t:"The treatment bypasses an inhibited pathway", correct:true, why:"Goes around a metabolic step the poison has blocked: warfarin blocks the regeneration of active vitamin K, so vitamin K itself is given."},
  {t:"The treatment prevents absorption of the poison", correct:false, why:"That is a toxicokinetic method."},
  {t:"The treatment chelates the poison", correct:false, why:"That is inactivation."}],
 teach:"The third approach to treating poisonings counteracts the poison's pharmacology by giving a drug with the opposite action, or one that bypasses the pathway the poison inhibited. Four subtopics, like the four for toxicokinetics: antagonist, agonist, removal from the active site, and bypassing an inhibited metabolic pathway.",
 quote:"sometimes we have. Uh, treatments that are agonists, or I'm sorry, antagonists is first. Then sometimes we have treatments that are agonists. Sometimes we have treatments that actually remove the poison from the active site, not interacting with the receptor, but actually removing it from a poison site. And then we have kind of a grab bag of pharmacologically based treatments that are when we will bypass an inhibited metabolic pathway. OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-040", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmrec", skill:"drug",
 concept:"naloxone-antagonist", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Naloxone for an opioid overdose belongs to which pharmacological category, and why?",
 options:[
  {t:"Receptor antagonist at the opioid receptor", correct:true, why:"Naloxone blocks the opioid receptor that the overdosed opioid, an agonist, is acting on."},
  {t:"Receptor agonist at the GABA receptor", correct:false, why:"Two errors. GABA is where flumazenil acts against benzodiazepines, and naloxone blocks rather than activates: the opioid is the agonist, so the antidote is the antagonist at the opioid receptor."},
  {t:"Removal of the opioid from its active site", correct:false, why:"That category is for pulling a poison off an enzyme, as with pralidoxime or methemoglobin."},
  {t:"Bypass of a pathway the opioid inhibited", correct:false, why:"That is the vitamin K category."},
  {t:"Inactivation of the opioid by an antibody", correct:false, why:"A monoclonal antibody for opioid overdose is not on the market."}],
 teach:"When the treatment is a receptor antagonist, it blocks the effect of a toxin that is acting as an agonist. Naloxone is the familiar example: an antagonist of the opioid receptor used to treat opioid overdoses.",
 quote:"a treatment where the receptor, I'm sorry, where the treatment is a receptor antagonist, using naloxone to treat an opioid overdose, right? Naloxone is basically an antagonist of an opioid receptor",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment is a receptor antagonist’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-041", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmrec", skill:"drug",
 concept:"flumazenil-gaba-antagonist", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In a benzodiazepine overdose treated with flumazenil, which drug is the agonist and which is the antagonist?",
 options:[
  {t:"The benzodiazepine agonizes the GABA receptor; flumazenil antagonizes it", correct:true, why:"The benzodiazepine is the overdose, flumazenil is the antidote, and the treatment is the antagonist; she said it backwards once and then confirmed she had it right the first time."},
  {t:"The benzodiazepine antagonizes the GABA receptor; flumazenil agonizes it", correct:false, why:"Her slip; she corrected it: the benzos agonize, flumazenil antagonizes."},
  {t:"Both are agonists; flumazenil is a weaker one", correct:false, why:"Flumazenil is an antagonist at the GABA (benzodiazepine) receptor."},
  {t:"Both are antagonists at different receptors", correct:false, why:"The benzodiazepine is the agonist."},
  {t:"Flumazenil removes the benzodiazepine from the active site of GABA", correct:false, why:"It is a receptor antagonist, not a removal treatment."}],
 teach:"Flumazenil is used for overdoses of benzodiazepines. The benzodiazepines are agonizing the GABA receptor, causing a lot of action there; flumazenil antagonizes that receptor. The treatment is the antagonist. She tangled the words on the slide, corrected herself, and asked for a better wording of the slide by email.",
 quote:"The benzos are the overdose, Flomazinil is your antidote, and your treatment is the antagonist. I had it right the first time. ... So, in this last example, flimazanil is the antagonist. It's antagonizing the GABA receptor, which the benzos are agonizing. OK?",
 note:"The transcript renders flumazenil as \"Flonazanil\", \"flomazanil\", \"Flomazinil\", \"flimazanil\". Her words \"Benzos are antagonists of GABA. The flomazinil is an agonist\" were a slip she withdrew in the next sentences; the slide says flumazenil is the antagonist at the GABA-A (benzodiazepine) receptor.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment is a receptor antagonist’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-042", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmrec", skill:"drug",
 concept:"atropine-organophosphate", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Why is atropine, a receptor antagonist, a treatment for organophosphate pesticide poisoning?",
 options:[
  {t:"Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, acetylcholine builds up; atropine antagonizes it", correct:true, why:"Too much acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft; atropine blocks its receptor."},
  {t:"Atropine reactivates the inhibited acetylcholinesterase", correct:false, why:"Pralidoxime does that; atropine works at the receptor."},
  {t:"Atropine agonizes the muscarinic receptor to replace the acetylcholine the organophosphate destroyed in the cleft", correct:false, why:"Acetylcholine is in excess, not lost, and atropine is an antagonist."},
  {t:"Atropine chelates the organophosphate in the blood", correct:false, why:"Chelators bind metals. Atropine works at the receptor, antagonizing the excess acetylcholine that builds up when the organophosphate inhibits acetylcholinesterase."},
  {t:"Atropine pulls the organophosphate off the enzyme onto itself", correct:false, why:"That is pralidoxime, and only before aging."}],
 teach:"Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine, so acetylcholine accumulates in the synaptic cleft and causes cholinergic excess. Atropine antagonizes acetylcholine at its receptor. This is one of two ways to treat an organophosphate exposure; the other is pralidoxime, which removes the organophosphate from the enzyme.",
 quote:"let's say you've got a person that's been exposed to organophosphate pesticides, right? They're inhibiting acetylcholinesterase. Therefore, you get an increase in acetylcholine in that synaptic cleft. So atropine is going to antagonize acetylcholine. OK.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment is a receptor antagonist’ (atropine); transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-043", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmrec", skill:"drug",
 concept:"physostigmine-agonist-category", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient has eaten jimsonweed, a belladonna alkaloid with anticholinergic effects, and is given physostigmine. In which category does this treatment fall, and why?",
 options:[
  {t:"Agonist category: it blocks acetylcholinesterase so acetylcholine rises", correct:true, why:"The toxin is an antagonist; physostigmine inhibits the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine, raising acetylcholine, so it acts like a receptor agonist."},
  {t:"Antagonist category: it blocks the muscarinic receptor", correct:false, why:"That would add to the anticholinergic effect."},
  {t:"Removal from the active site: it pulls the alkaloid off the muscarinic receptor", correct:false, why:"It works through the enzyme, not by displacing the alkaloid."},
  {t:"Bypass category: it supplies a cofactor the alkaloid depleted", correct:false, why:"Physostigmine does not replace anything the alkaloid used up. It blocks acetylcholinesterase so acetylcholine rises, and that is why it acts like a receptor agonist against an anticholinergic toxin."},
  {t:"Inactivation: it binds the alkaloid in the blood", correct:false, why:"Physostigmine is not an antibody or chelator."}],
 teach:"When the treatment is a receptor agonist, it blocks the effect of a toxin that is acting as an antagonist. The belladonna alkaloids, jimsonweed being the classic example, cause anticholinergic effects. Physostigmine blocks acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine increases and acts on the receptors the alkaloid is blocking; the slide's words are that it therefore acts like a receptor agonist.",
 quote:"Now our treatment is a receptor agonist. The treatment is physostigmine, and the classic example is to treat. Exposure to plants that are what we call the belladonna alkaloids, like jimsonweed is the classic example of it, they cause anticholinergic effects. And so physostigma is gonna agonize those receptors, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment is a receptor agonist’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-044", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmrec", skill:"apply",
 concept:"belladonna-mydriasis", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which eye sign do the belladonna alkaloids such as jimsonweed produce, the sign behind the name?",
 options:[
  {t:"Dilated pupils (mydriasis)", correct:true, why:"Belladonna, beautiful lady: the plants make the eyes dilate."},
  {t:"Constricted pupils (miosis)", correct:false, why:"She said she mixes the two terms up; the sign she named is dilation."},
  {t:"Loss of retinal receptors and blindness", correct:false, why:"That is methanol's formic acid."},
  {t:"Tearing (lacrimation)", correct:false, why:"Tearing is a cholinergic sign, the opposite picture."},
  {t:"Blue staining of the sclera", correct:false, why:"Methylene blue stains; it has nothing to do with belladonna."}],
 teach:"The belladonna alkaloids are anticholinergic, and the classic sign, the reason for the name, is big dilated pupils: mydriasis. She admitted mixing up the terms for constricting and dilating but was clear that jimsonweed makes the eyes dilate. Physostigmine is the treatment.",
 quote:"Belladonna alkaloids, anybody know why they call them the Belladonna alkaloids, you get those big. Eyes, your, what do you call it, um, when the pupils dilate. Middresses, right? ... So those jimsonweed plants, which is a belladonna alkaloid, will make your eyes dilate.",
 note:"The transcript renders mydriasis as \"Middresses\".",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment is a receptor agonist’; transcript 10/19"},

/* ---------------- Pharmacological treatment II ---------------- */
{id:"L16-045", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmsite", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cyanide-removal-from-active-site", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the cyanide scheme, which step is the example of a treatment that removes the toxin from its active site?",
 options:[
  {t:"Methemoglobin pulling cyanide off cytochrome c oxidase", correct:true, why:"Creating methemoglobin was the means; the removal of cyanide from the enzyme's active site is this step."},
  {t:"Rhodanese moving cyanide onto thiosulfate", correct:false, why:"That is the enhance-metabolism step."},
  {t:"Methylene blue reducing methemoglobin", correct:false, why:"That repairs the hemoglobin; the cyanide is already off the enzyme."},
  {t:"Thiosulfate-bound cyanide leaving in the urine", correct:false, why:"That is the end of the clearance step. By then the cyanide is long off the enzyme; the removal from the active site happened when methemoglobin pulled it off."},
  {t:"High-concentration oxygen displacing cyanide from the heme of hemoglobin", correct:false, why:"Oxygen displacement from hemoglobin is the carbon monoxide treatment."}],
 teach:"She went back to the cyanide figure to show this category. Cytochrome c oxidase is the target; cyanide binds it; methemoglobin, created with amyl nitrite, pulls the cyanide off the enzyme onto itself. That is removing the poison from the active site, after which the cells can respire again, the cyanide still has to be cleared (rhodanese and thiosulfate), and the hemoglobin has to be regenerated (methylene blue).",
 quote:"And then we are creating the methemoglobin, and that methemoglobin is pulling cyanide off of the cytochrome C oxidase onto itself. So this is removing the poison from the active site. We had to create the methemoglobin to do that. But we are removing the cyanide from the active site.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slides ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment Removes Toxin from Active Site’, ‘Toxicokinetic-Based Treatment Cyanide example’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-046", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmsite", skill:"figure",
 concept:"pralidoxime-removes-op", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the pralidoxime figure, what does pralidoxime do to an organophosphate bound to acetylcholinesterase?",
 options:[
  {t:"Pulls the organophosphate off the enzyme onto itself for elimination", correct:true, why:"The organophosphate on the enzyme is inactivating it; pralidoxime takes the organophosphate onto itself, restoring the active site, and the complex can be eliminated."},
  {t:"Blocks the muscarinic receptor against the excess acetylcholine in the cleft", correct:false, why:"That is atropine."},
  {t:"Breaks down the excess acetylcholine in the cleft", correct:false, why:"Acetylcholinesterase does that once it is freed."},
  {t:"Induces the synthesis of new acetylcholinesterase", correct:false, why:"Induction is too slow for a poisoning."},
  {t:"Binds acetylcholine so it cannot reach the receptor", correct:false, why:"Pralidoxime acts on the enzyme, not the transmitter."}],
 teach:"The bottom panel of the figure walks through the organophosphate bound to acetylcholinesterase, inactivating it, and pralidoxime pulling the organophosphate off the enzyme onto itself so that it can be eliminated and the enzyme's active site is restored. It is a second way to treat organophosphate exposures, alongside atropine for the acetylcholine excess.",
 quote:"One of the things that we can do is to pull off the organophosphate by using pralodoxine. What you'll see here is, here's pralodoxine here. And it's gonna pull off the organophosphate and put it onto itself. And when it does that, it can be eliminated. OK? So, pralodoxine is a second way we can treat organophosphate exposures.",
 note:"The transcript renders pralidoxime as \"pralodoxine\", \"Praludoxine\", \"pralodoxium\".",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment Pralidoxime example’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-047", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmsite", skill:"apply",
 concept:"organophosphate-aging", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Pralidoxime is given to a patient poisoned by an organophosphate, yet the acetylcholinesterase stays inhibited. What has happened?",
 options:[
  {t:"The organophosphate has aged on the enzyme", correct:true, why:"Part of the bound organophosphate fell off, and the aged structure is permanently bound; pralidoxime cannot pull it onto itself."},
  {t:"Pralidoxime was given before atropine", correct:false, why:"Order was not the issue; soldiers' kits mix both."},
  {t:"The patient's acetylcholinesterase is genetically different", correct:false, why:"Aging is a property of the organophosphate, not the patient."},
  {t:"The organophosphate was inhaled rather than swallowed", correct:false, why:"Route does not decide it. Aging depends on the organophosphate's chemical structure: if a piece of it falls off after it binds acetylcholinesterase, the enzyme is bound permanently and pralidoxime cannot pull it off."},
  {t:"The dose of pralidoxime was too small to antagonize the receptor", correct:false, why:"Pralidoxime does not act at the receptor at all."}],
 teach:"Some organophosphates have a structure such that, after binding acetylcholinesterase, a part of the phosphate group falls off; in the figure the carbon-oxygen-hydrogen piece is lost and a phosphate with a double-bonded oxygen, a hydroxyl and a methyl remains. That is aging. Once it has happened the enzyme is permanently bound by the aged organophosphate and pralidoxime cannot remove it, so pralidoxime does not work for every organophosphate exposure.",
 quote:"Some organophosphates have a structure such that after they've bound to the to the acetylcholinesterase, a part of the phosphate falls off. Once that happens. Pralodoxine is not able to pull off that organophosphate onto itself, OK? Therefore, you end up with the acetylcholinesterase bound permanently by that aged organophosphate, OK? And there you go, you can't treat it using the Praludoxine.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment Pralidoxime example’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-048", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmsite", skill:"apply",
 concept:"aging-depends-on-structure", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A student asked whether certain conditions sway an organophosphate to age. What decides it?",
 options:[
  {t:"The chemical structure of the organophosphate", correct:true, why:"Whether that little bit breaks off is set by the compound's structure; not every organophosphate has a structure that loses it."},
  {t:"The dose that was absorbed", correct:false, why:"Dose does not decide aging; structure does. Only an organophosphate whose structure loses a small piece after binding the enzyme ages, whatever the amount."},
  {t:"The patient's body temperature", correct:false, why:"Nothing about the patient decides aging. Asked exactly this, she answered that it is the chemical structure of the organophosphate: whether that little bit breaks off."},
  {t:"How long the trip to the hospital took, by itself", correct:false, why:"Time matters only for compounds whose structure can age; the structure decides."},
  {t:"Whether atropine was given first", correct:false, why:"Atropine treats the acetylcholine excess and does not affect aging."}],
 teach:"Aging is decided by the organophosphate's chemistry, not by conditions in the patient. Some organophosphates have a structure that loses a small piece after binding acetylcholinesterase; the aged form is then bound permanently, and pralidoxime can no longer pull it onto itself. Organophosphates without that structure do not age, so pralidoxime can still remove them. Atropine blocks the excess acetylcholine at the receptor, so it acts whether or not aging has occurred. Soldiers' field kits carry both.",
 quote:"Certain conditions that sway aging to happen or not, it's, it's not like, it's, it's the structure of the organophosphate. Yeah, it's the chemical structure of the organophosphate, whether or not that little bit breaks off, and then pralodoxium can't grab it. ... they'll have a kit with them, right? That has, has both compounds, both, both therapies, right? Yeah, they mix it in one, yeah, yeah.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment Pralidoxime example’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-049", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmsite", skill:"drug", lowYield:true,
 concept:"co-oxygen-displacement", tags:["smith"], source:"slide",
 stem:"How is carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning treated, and in which pharmacological category does that treatment fall?",
 options:[
  {t:"High concentrations of oxygen; removal of the toxin from the active site", correct:true, why:"Oxygen displaces CO from the heme of hemoglobin, the slide's second example of removing a poison from its active site."},
  {t:"Methylene blue; bypass of an inhibited pathway", correct:false, why:"Methylene blue belongs to the cyanide scheme."},
  {t:"Amyl nitrite; receptor antagonist", correct:false, why:"Amyl nitrite makes methemoglobin for cyanide."},
  {t:"Hemodialysis of the blood; extracorporeal enhancement of elimination for severe cases", correct:false, why:"CO is on hemoglobin; the treatment displaces it with oxygen."},
  {t:"Sodium bicarbonate; ion trapping", correct:false, why:"CO is not a weak acid in the urine."}],
 teach:"The slide on removing a toxin from its active site lists three examples: cyanide (methemoglobin pulling it off cytochrome c oxidase), carbon monoxide (treated with high concentrations of oxygen to displace CO from the heme of hemoglobin), and organophosphates (pralidoxime restoring the active site of acetylcholinesterase).",
 quote:"And I'm gonna go back to our copper monoxide exposures in just a minute here, but let's walk our way through organophosphate exposures, OK?",
 note:"Low yield: the carbon monoxide bullet was not reached on 10/19. Keyed from the slide: she said she would come back to carbon monoxide (\"copper monoxide\" in the transcript) but did not; the slide was displayed and the cyanide and organophosphate bullets on it were taught. Lift the flag if she covers CO on 10/20.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment Removes Toxin from Active Site’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-050", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmsite", skill:"figure",
 concept:"warfarin-epoxide-reductase", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the warfarin figure, which enzyme does warfarin inhibit, and what is the consequence?",
 options:[
  {t:"Epoxide reductase, so active vitamin K is not regenerated", correct:true, why:"Her answer to the room: the epoxide reductase. Less vitamin K is reformed, so fewer coagulation factors are made."},
  {t:"The vitamin K dependent carboxylase directly, so no factors are carboxylated", correct:false, why:"It would be easier to understand if it did, she said, but it does not; it works through the vitamin K supply."},
  {t:"Alcohol dehydrogenase, so formic acid is not formed", correct:false, why:"That is the methanol antidote story."},
  {t:"Cytochrome c oxidase, so ATP production stops", correct:false, why:"That is cyanide."},
  {t:"Glutathione S-transferase, so NAPQI is not conjugated", correct:false, why:"Acetaminophen scheme."}],
 teach:"Normal physiology: some coagulation factors become active only after carboxylation by the vitamin K dependent carboxylase, which needs active vitamin K as a cofactor. Using it turns vitamin K into an inactive epoxide, and epoxide reductase regenerates the active form, round and round. Warfarin inhibits the epoxide reductase, so less active vitamin K is reformed and fewer active coagulation factors are made: an indirect mechanism of anticoagulation.",
 quote:"What enzyme is it inhibiting? Yeah, the epoxide reductase. So the mechanism of action of warfarin in this scheme of creating coagulation factors is it inhibits the activity of the epoxide reductase. Therefore, you reduce the amount of vitamin K being reformed. Therefore, if you don't have as much active vitamin K, you don't make as much coagulation factors. ... It would be nice if warfarin was acting on that vitamin K dependent carboxylase, right? It would make the mechanism of action so much easier to understand, but it's not.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment by-passes an inhibited pathway’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-051", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmsite", skill:"drug",
 concept:"vitamin-k-bypass", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What is given for a warfarin overdose, and why is it classed as bypassing an inhibited pathway?",
 options:[
  {t:"Vitamin K; it replaces what the blocked recycling step cannot make", correct:true, why:"The epoxide reductase is inhibited, so active vitamin K cannot be regenerated; giving vitamin K as a drug goes around that block and lets coagulation factors be made."},
  {t:"Vitamin K; it antagonizes warfarin at its receptor", correct:false, why:"Warfarin acts on an enzyme, and vitamin K does not compete with it."},
  {t:"Vitamin K; it chelates warfarin in the plasma", correct:false, why:"Vitamin K does not bind warfarin. Warfarin blocks epoxide reductase, so active vitamin K is not regenerated; giving vitamin K supplies the active cofactor directly and goes around the block."},
  {t:"Epoxide reductase; it replaces the inhibited enzyme", correct:false, why:"The enzyme is not given; its product is."},
  {t:"An inducer of the carboxylase; it makes more coagulation factors within hours", correct:false, why:"Induction is too slow, and the carboxylase is not the problem."}],
 teach:"Too much warfarin blocks the regeneration of active vitamin K, so the patient does not make enough active coagulation factors. The antidote is vitamin K itself, given as a drug, which bypasses the blocked metabolic scheme. Her story: a liver-transplant-clinic patient on warfarin, bruised all over her arms, got a vitamin K injection after she alerted the nurses.",
 quote:"Anybody know what the the antidote for a warfarin overdose is? What might we do? Can you look at this scheme and see anything in this scheme that we could give to a patient? To help Get a little more coagulation factor made if we have over anticoagulated them. Vitamin K, right? We will do this. We will give vitamin K. ... Kind of bypassing this blocked metabolic scheme. OK.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment by-passes an inhibited pathway’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-052", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmsite", skill:"apply",
 concept:"over-anticoagulation-signs", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which signs and symptoms point to a patient being over-anticoagulated on warfarin?",
 options:[
  {t:"Bruising, GI bleeds, hemorrhagic stroke", correct:true, why:"Bruising is the most obvious; GI bleeds and a hemorrhagic stroke are the bad outcomes."},
  {t:"Dilated pupils and flushing", correct:false, why:"Anticholinergic signs."},
  {t:"Tingling of the hands and feet", correct:false, why:"Myelinopathy, from 10/14."},
  {t:"Blindness from retinal damage", correct:false, why:"Retinal damage and blindness come from formic acid in methanol poisoning. Over-anticoagulation shows as bleeding instead: bruising, GI bleeds, a hemorrhagic stroke."},
  {t:"Salivation, tearing and gastric cramping", correct:false, why:"Cholinergic excess."}],
 teach:"Warfarin inhibits epoxide reductase, so less active vitamin K is regenerated and fewer active coagulation factors are made. Too much warfarin pushes this too far: the blood does not clot well, so the patient bleeds. Bruising is the most obvious sign; GI bleeds and a hemorrhagic stroke are the dangerous outcomes. The treatment is vitamin K, which bypasses the blocked step.",
 quote:"What's the risk of being over anticoagulated? What signs and symptoms might you see? Most obviously we be bruising. Sometimes people can have GI bleeds, right? Really bad thing, maybe they'll have a hemorrhagic stroke, right?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment by-passes an inhibited pathway’; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-053", dupOf:"L16-039", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"pharmsite", skill:"drug", type:"match",
 concept:"pharmacological-examples-table", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Match each pharmacological treatment with how it counteracts the poison.",
 left:["Naloxone (opioid overdose)", "Flumazenil (benzodiazepine overdose)", "Atropine (organophosphate)", "Physostigmine (belladonna alkaloids)", "Pralidoxime (organophosphate)", "Vitamin K (warfarin)"],
 right:["Antagonist at the opioid receptor", "Antagonist at the GABA (benzodiazepine) receptor", "Antagonist of the excess acetylcholine", "Agonist category: blocks acetylcholinesterase to raise acetylcholine", "Removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase", "Bypasses the inhibited vitamin K recycling pathway"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Naloxone (opioid overdose)", r:"Antagonist at the opioid receptor", why:"Treatment is a receptor antagonist."},
  {l:"Flumazenil (benzodiazepine overdose)", r:"Antagonist at the GABA (benzodiazepine) receptor", why:"Treatment is a receptor antagonist; the benzodiazepine is the agonist."},
  {l:"Atropine (organophosphate)", r:"Antagonist of the excess acetylcholine", why:"Treatment is a receptor antagonist; the organophosphate raised acetylcholine."},
  {l:"Physostigmine (belladonna alkaloids)", r:"Agonist category: blocks acetylcholinesterase to raise acetylcholine", why:"Treatment is a receptor agonist, against an anticholinergic toxin."},
  {l:"Pralidoxime (organophosphate)", r:"Removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase", why:"Treatment removes the toxin from the active site; fails after aging."},
  {l:"Vitamin K (warfarin)", r:"Bypasses the inhibited vitamin K recycling pathway", why:"Treatment bypasses an inhibited pathway."}],
 teach:"Each category is defined by how the antidote counters the poison. Antagonists block a receptor that is being over-activated: naloxone at the opioid receptor, flumazenil at the GABA receptor, atropine against the acetylcholine that builds up when organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase. Physostigmine counts as an agonist because it blocks acetylcholinesterase, raising acetylcholine against an anticholinergic toxin. Pralidoxime pulls the organophosphate off the enzyme's active site. Vitamin K bypasses the recycling step warfarin blocks.",
 quote:"we only had 4 or 5 examples of kinetically-based treatments and about 4 or 5 examples of these pharmacologically-based treatments, right? So, not too many to learn yet, OK. But so the point of that lecture was to talk about the bigger concept",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slides ‘Pharmacological Treatment: Treatment is a receptor antagonist’, ‘Treatment is a receptor agonist’, ‘Treatment Removes Toxin from Active Site’, ‘Treatment by-passes an inhibited pathway’; transcript 10/19"},

/* ---------------- The guideline discussion ---------------- */
{id:"L16-054", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"recall", readingId:"aapcc-bb",
 concept:"guidelines-2005-still-followed", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker out-of-hospital guidelines were published in 2005. Where, and are they still current?",
 options:[
  {t:"Clinical Toxicology; no newer ones, and 2005 is still what is followed", correct:true, why:"No new ways of treating these overdoses have come up; poison center staff have these documents memorized."},
  {t:"The New England Journal of Medicine; replaced every five years", correct:false, why:"The guidelines appear in Clinical Toxicology, and they have not been replaced: no new ways of treating these overdoses have come up, so the 2005 versions are still followed."},
  {t:"DiPiro chapter e9; updated with each edition", correct:false, why:"DiPiro is the textbook; the guidelines are journal papers."},
  {t:"Clinical Toxicology; withdrawn once randomized clinical trials of overdose were run", correct:false, why:"Trials of poisoning are not run; the documents stand."},
  {t:"The FDA label of each drug; revised yearly", correct:false, why:"They are consensus guidelines, not labels."}],
 teach:"The evidence-based consensus guidelines for out-of-hospital management are published in Clinical Toxicology; the beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker ones date from 2005. They are still followed because nothing has changed in how these overdoses are treated; they would be rewritten only if something such as a new antidote came along. Poison control center staff, who answer the calls, have these documents memorized.",
 quote:"They are published in the Journal of Clinical Toxicology, and they have not done any new ones recently, so you can see that both the ones, uh, the beta blocker and the calcium channel blockers were published in 2005, and that's OK. They haven't come up with new ways of treating these overdoses. What they published in 2005 is still what they follow, OK. ... people that work in these poison control centers, to become qualified to work there and answer phone calls, they would basically have these documents memorized",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 guidelines; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-055", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"term", readingId:"aapcc-ccb",
 concept:"evidence-based-means-ranked", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"What makes the out-of-hospital guidance documents evidence-based?",
 options:[
  {t:"The data behind them are ranked, and the strength of each recommendation is ranked", correct:true, why:"The authors know the data they are using, rank it, and rank the strength of the recommendations they make; that is what evidence-based means."},
  {t:"They were written from randomized clinical trials in which volunteers were overdosed", correct:false, why:"No such trials are possible; the data are published cases and case series."},
  {t:"They were approved by the FDA", correct:false, why:"Evidence-based describes how the document was built, not who approved it: the authors ranked the published case data and ranked the strength of each recommendation."},
  {t:"Every author is a poison center director", correct:false, why:"Evidence-based describes the method, not the authors: the published cases were pulled and evaluated, the data ranked, and each recommendation's strength ranked."},
  {t:"They are rewritten every year from new data", correct:false, why:"They have not been redone since 2005."}],
 teach:"No one can run trials that poison volunteers, so the authors went into the literature, pulled case reports and anything else published, and evaluated it under the medical guidelines for evidence-based documents. They ranked the data, and they ranked the strength of each recommendation; that ranking is what evidence-based means. The class had met this in the drug information course.",
 quote:"they know the data that they're using. They rank the data that they have to create these documents, and they rank the strength of the recommendations they make in these documents. That's what is meant by evidence-based.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Guidelines for Overdoses’; AAPCC 2005 guidelines; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-056", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"apply", readingId:"aapcc-ccb",
 concept:"home-no-emesis-charcoal-hospital", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A family member finds someone at home who has swallowed a handful of calcium channel blocker or beta blocker tablets. Should they make the person vomit, and what about activated charcoal?",
 options:[
  {t:"No vomiting; consider charcoal but do not delay transport", correct:true, why:"Her key: do not induce emesis; consider activated charcoal if there are no contraindications; do not delay transport to the hospital to administer charcoal. On 10/19 she added that charcoal is something done at the hospital."},
  {t:"Induce vomiting with ipecac, then give charcoal at home", correct:false, why:"No emesis, and transport is not delayed to give charcoal."},
  {t:"Give charcoal at home through a nasogastric tube", correct:false, why:"We are not sophisticated enough to do that at home without hurting somebody."},
  {t:"Induce vomiting at home only when the product was extended-release", correct:false, why:"Vomiting is not induced for either formulation."},
  {t:"Do nothing for 6 hours and then decide", correct:false, why:"The call to the poison center and 911 is made at once; the windows decide where the patient goes, not whether to call."}],
 teach:"The first question on her key: what to do to reduce absorption. Her key: do not induce emesis; consider activated charcoal if there are no contraindications; do not delay transport to the hospital to give it. On 10/19 she said charcoal is done at the hospital and that no one should try to make the person vomit at home. The out-of-hospital guidelines are about what a family member and the poison center do before the hospital.",
 quote:"What should you do to try to prevent reabsorption of these either calcium channel blockers or beta blockers? What should you, what should you do? Yeah, charcoal, right? Do you do that at home? No, that is something that they'll do when they get to the hospital, OK? ... At home, do you encourage somebody to get the person to vomit? We don't recommend that anymore, right? It's too dangerous, right? They might, they might aspirate.",
 note:"Her key (Readingguideforoutofhospitalguidelines2026KEY.docx): do not induce emesis; consider activated charcoal if there are no contraindications; do not delay transport to the hospital to administer charcoal. Her 10/19 words place charcoal at the hospital; both are kept.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 guidelines; Readingguideforoutofhospitalguidelines2026KEY.docx; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-057", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"apply", readingId:"aapcc-bb",
 concept:"ir-six-hour-window", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"An asymptomatic patient took an immediate-release calcium channel blocker, or an immediate-release beta blocker other than sotalol, and was found within 6 hours of ingestion. What does the guideline say to do, and what if it had been more than 6 hours?",
 options:[
  {t:"Get them to the hospital; after 6 hours without symptoms they can probably be managed at home", correct:true, why:"Within 6 hours they are still inside the time when they could respond badly; after 6 hours an immediate-release drug should have been absorbed and shown symptoms, so an asymptomatic patient is OK, watched at home. Her key: after 6 hours, call the poison center; they can probably be managed at home, and the poison center will inform you of follow-up procedures."},
  {t:"Manage at home; after 6 hours send them to the hospital", correct:false, why:"Backwards. Within 6 hours an immediate-release drug may still be absorbing, so the asymptomatic patient goes to the hospital; past 6 hours symptoms would already have appeared, so a patient with none can probably stay home."},
  {t:"Wait for symptoms, then call 911; the window does not matter", correct:false, why:"Waiting ignores the window: within 6 hours an asymptomatic patient can still respond badly, so they go to the hospital now, not after symptoms start."},
  {t:"Give cranberry juice and observe; after 6 hours give charcoal at home", correct:false, why:"Cranberry juice is the weak urine-acidifying example from ion trapping, and charcoal is a hospital step, not a home one. The guideline's answer is hospital within 6 hours, home with the poison center after."},
  {t:"Hospital in either case; an immediate-release ingestion is never managed at home, symptoms or not", correct:false, why:"Past 6 hours and asymptomatic, the guideline says they are going to be OK."}],
 teach:"For an immediate-release product the window is 6 hours, the same for both classes. Found within 6 hours and asymptomatic: get them to the hospital, since the drug could still take effect. Found after 6 hours and still asymptomatic: all the drug should have been absorbed and symptoms would have appeared, so they can be managed at home, watched, with the poison center on the line. Her key excepts sotalol from the beta-blocker immediate-release rule. On 10/20 she added that if the amount taken is unknown, the patient probably needs to go to the hospital.",
 quote:"If they're within 6 hours of digestion and they're not symptomatic yet, what do you do? You're gonna get them to the hospital, why? Because if, if, if they haven't passed the 6 hour mark for an immediate release. Exposure, then you're still within the time when they could really badly respond, OK? ... if it's been 6 hours and they don't have any symptoms, they're OK. You're gonna stay by them and watch them, but they're gonna be OK. You don't need to take them into the hospital. OK.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 guidelines; Readingguideforoutofhospitalguidelines2026KEY.docx; transcript 10/19; transcript 10/20 (amount unknown)"},

{id:"L16-058", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"apply", readingId:"aapcc-ccb",
 concept:"ccb-modified-release-18h", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"An asymptomatic patient took a modified-release calcium channel blocker other than verapamil. Found within what time of ingestion must they still go to the hospital?",
 options:[
  {t:"18 hours", correct:true, why:"Her key for a modified-release calcium channel blocker, except verapamil: within 18 hours, get them in."},
  {t:"6 hours", correct:false, why:"That is the immediate-release window."},
  {t:"8 hours", correct:false, why:"That is the sustained-release beta blocker window."},
  {t:"24 hours", correct:false, why:"That is modified-release verapamil."},
  {t:"48 hours", correct:false, why:"The longest window in either guideline is 24 hours, for modified-release verapamil; the other modified-release calcium channel blockers use 18."}],
 teach:"Modified-release products clump in the gut and can break apart later, releasing a large dose, so an asymptomatic patient is not safe until that window has passed. For calcium channel blockers other than verapamil the window is 18 hours: found within 18 hours, get them to the hospital; found after 18 hours and still asymptomatic, call the poison center, and they can probably be managed at home. Sustained-release beta blockers use 8 hours; modified-release verapamil, 24.",
 quote:"If it's a modified release, except for verapamil, and you found them within 18 hours of ingestion and they are not symptomatic, they are asymptomatic. What do you need to do? Yeah, they're asymptomatic, and they ingested the modified release product, and you found them within 18 hours of ingestion. You need to get him in.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 calcium channel blocker guideline; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-059", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"apply", readingId:"aapcc-bb",
 concept:"bb-sustained-release-8h", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"An asymptomatic patient took a sustained-release beta blocker. Found within what time of ingestion must they still go to the hospital?",
 options:[
  {t:"8 hours", correct:true, why:"Her key: for a sustained-release beta blocker the window is 8 hours."},
  {t:"6 hours", correct:false, why:"Immediate-release window."},
  {t:"18 hours", correct:false, why:"Modified-release calcium channel blocker, not verapamil."},
  {t:"24 hours", correct:false, why:"Modified-release verapamil."},
  {t:"12 hours", correct:false, why:"Not a window in her key; the sustained-release beta blocker window is 8 hours. Her key mentions sotalol only as an exception to the immediate-release rule."}],
 teach:"The sustained-release beta blocker window is 8 hours, shorter than the 18 hours for modified-release calcium channel blockers. The reason for any extended window is that these tablets clump and can break apart later; the reason for the class difference is in the case data, which speak to the different mechanisms of the two drug classes.",
 quote:"Same thing with the beta blockers, a sustained release product, but, but the time frame for this is within 8 hours, right? You got to get him to the hospital.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 beta-blocker guideline; Readingguideforoutofhospitalguidelines2026KEY.docx; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-060", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"apply", readingId:"aapcc-ccb",
 concept:"verapamil-modified-release-24h", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Modified-release verapamil is treated differently from the other calcium channel blockers. Within what time of ingestion does an asymptomatic patient still go to the hospital?",
 options:[
  {t:"24 hours", correct:true, why:"Verapamil is really different: within 24 hours, hospital; after 24 hours and asymptomatic, probably OK with a call to the poison center."},
  {t:"6 hours", correct:false, why:"Immediate-release window."},
  {t:"8 hours", correct:false, why:"Sustained-release beta blocker."},
  {t:"18 hours", correct:false, why:"The other modified-release calcium channel blockers."},
  {t:"There is no window; verapamil always goes to the hospital", correct:false, why:"After 24 hours without symptoms they are probably OK."}],
 teach:"Among the calcium channel blockers, modified-release verapamil carries the longest window: 24 hours. Found within 24 hours, even asymptomatic, the patient goes to the hospital; found after 24 hours and asymptomatic, they are probably OK, but the poison center is still called. On 10/20 she gave the reason: verapamil has multiple mechanisms and is not as clean a calcium channel blocker as the others, so the concern lasts longer.",
 quote:"If you found a person that's ingested modified release verapamil, and you found them within 24 hours of ingestion. You still get them to the hospital, right? Verapamil is really different, OK? If you find them after 24 hours and they are non-systematic symptomatic, they're probably OK.",
 note:"Reading her key she first said modified-release verapamil found \"after 18 hours\" is probably OK, then gave the verapamil window as 24 hours (within 24 hours hospital; after 24 hours probably OK); the calcium channel blocker guideline gives 24 hours for modified-release verapamil. Keyed on 24 hours. The transcript renders asymptomatic as \"non-systematic symptomatic\".",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 calcium channel blocker guideline; transcript 10/19; transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L16-061", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"apply", readingId:"aapcc-ccb",
 concept:"extended-release-clumping", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Why does an overdose of an extended-release product need a longer observation window than an immediate-release one?",
 options:[
  {t:"The tablets clump in the gut and can break apart later, releasing a huge dose at once", correct:true, why:"The chemistry of the formulations makes them stick together; while the clump is intact the patient may have no symptoms, and when it breaks up a huge absorption follows."},
  {t:"Extended-release tablets are more potent per milligram", correct:false, why:"Potency is not the issue; timing of absorption is."},
  {t:"They are absorbed faster than immediate-release tablets, so symptoms take longer to clear", correct:false, why:"They are absorbed more slowly, and the clumping adds a delayed surge."},
  {t:"Charcoal cannot adsorb them", correct:false, why:"Charcoal is considered for both formulations. The longer window comes from the extended-release tablets clumping into a bezoar that can break apart later and release a large dose."},
  {t:"They undergo enterohepatic recirculation", correct:false, why:"Recirculation is a multiple-dose charcoal story, not the window story."}],
 teach:"An extended-release dose takes longer to absorb, which is true in overdose as well. The bigger problem is that these formulations clump in the stomach or small intestine (a bezoar, the word she tied to Harry Potter). A patient found asymptomatic may still be inside the window during which the clump could break apart and release the whole dose at once, which could be very dangerous; they should be in the hospital before that happens.",
 quote:"an issue with overdosing on these modified release or extended release or sustained release products is that the chemistry of those formulations is such that they often clump in the stomach or in the small intestine. You guys know what the word bazo is? Think Harry Potter, right? ... you are still within the window that that clump of tablets could break apart, and all of a sudden you're gonna get a huge absorption of that medication. Which could be really, really dangerous.",
 note:"The transcript renders bezoar as \"bazo\".",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 guidelines; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-062", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"tell", readingId:"aapcc-bb",
 concept:"bb-vs-ccb-window-difference", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers are both cardiac drugs, yet their extended-release windows differ (8 versus 18 hours). What explains the difference?",
 options:[
  {t:"The case data differ, reflecting the two classes' different mechanisms of action", correct:true, why:"The authors pooled all the overdose cases, analyzed them and set the windows; the different time frames speak to how calcium channel blockers and beta blockers act on the heart, detail for the cardio module."},
  {t:"Beta blockers are all immediate-release", correct:false, why:"Sustained-release beta blockers exist; that is the 8-hour window."},
  {t:"Calcium channel blockers are not adsorbed by charcoal", correct:false, why:"Charcoal is used for both."},
  {t:"The two guidelines were written in different years from different poison center data", correct:false, why:"Both were published in 2005."},
  {t:"Verapamil is a beta blocker", correct:false, why:"Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker with its own 24-hour window."}],
 teach:"Asked why the time frames differ, the room offered kinetics and the clumping of tablets; she accepted that the data show it, and added that the difference between the two classes speaks to their mechanisms of action, which the class will understand after the cardio module. The immediate- versus extended-release difference is the clumping; the beta-blocker versus calcium channel blocker difference is mechanism.",
 quote:"What's the difference? Why is there a different time frame? Yeah, the kinetics of it, the nature of the tablets clumping together, it's what the data has been showing us, OK? ... slightly different time frames that speaks to the mechanism of action of calcium channel blockers versus beta blockers, OK",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 guidelines; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-063", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"apply", readingId:"aapcc-bb",
 concept:"ambulance-not-car", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The patient needs to get to the hospital. Should the family drive them, or call an ambulance, and why?",
 options:[
  {t:"Ambulance; it carries what is needed if the patient crashes", correct:true, why:"IVs, pressors and paddles are in the ambulance; stuck in traffic in a car with a crashing patient, you are out of luck."},
  {t:"Family car; it is faster than waiting for an ambulance", correct:false, why:"Speed is not the issue; equipment is."},
  {t:"Either; the choice does not matter inside the 6-hour window", correct:false, why:"The vehicle matters whatever the window: any patient sent in may crash on the way, and only the ambulance has IVs, pressors and paddles."},
  {t:"Family car for immediate-release, ambulance for extended-release", correct:false, why:"Formulation changes the window, not the transport: any patient who needs the hospital can crash on the way, so all go by ambulance."},
  {t:"Ambulance only for verapamil", correct:false, why:"Every patient who needs the hospital, after either drug class, goes by ambulance, because any of them may crash on the way."}],
 teach:"Question 3 on her key, the same for both classes: call an ambulance. Beta blocker and calcium channel blocker overdoses can crash, and the ambulance carries the IV fluids, the pressors and the paddles to deal with it. A family car stuck in traffic has none of that.",
 quote:"if you need to get them to the hospital, should you put them in your car and drive them? You need to call an ambulance. Why? Cuz they've got the stuff on the animals to help them if they start. Crashing. OK, you don't have that in your car. You get stuck in traffic trying to get them to the hospital and they start to crash. You are out of luck, you don't have any IVs to hang, you don't have any pressers to hang, OK, you don't have any paddles.",
 note:"\"the stuff on the animals\" in the transcript is the stuff on the ambulance; \"pressers\" is pressors.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 guidelines; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-064", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"tell", readingId:"aapcc-bb",
 concept:"follow-up-interval-difference", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"How do the two guidelines differ on follow-up of a patient managed at home?",
 options:[
  {t:"Calcium channel blockers: the poison center's judgment; beta blockers: up to 12 to 24 hours, also at its judgment", correct:true, why:"Slightly different details in the follow-up, reflecting what the data showed for the two kinds of cases."},
  {t:"Both: a single call at 6 hours", correct:false, why:"Neither guideline sets a fixed call; 6 hours is the immediate-release window for going to the hospital. Calcium channel blocker follow-up is left to the poison center's judgment, and beta-blocker follow-up runs for up to 12 to 24 hours."},
  {t:"No follow-up is needed if the patient is asymptomatic", correct:false, why:"Follow-up is part of both guidelines."},
  {t:"Beta blockers: a fixed call at 48 hours; calcium channel blockers: a fixed call at 6 hours, regardless of the poison center's judgment", correct:false, why:"Both guidelines leave follow-up to the poison center's judgment; only the beta-blocker one names a range, calls for up to 12 to 24 hours. No fixed 48-hour or 6-hour call appears in either."},
  {t:"Follow-up only after a verapamil ingestion", correct:false, why:"Follow-up applies to both classes."}],
 teach:"For calcium channel blockers the guideline leaves the follow-up interval to the judgment of the poison center; for beta blockers follow-up calls are made at appropriate intervals for up to 12 to 24 hours, also at the poison center's judgment. The small difference comes from the data behind each document.",
 quote:"for the calcium channel blockers, they say follow-up will be determined based on the judgment of the poison center, and for the guidance document for the beta blockers, um, uh, usually the interval is about 2012 to 24 hours based on the judgment of the poison center.",
 note:"The transcript's \"2012 to 24 hours\" is read as 12 to 24 hours. Her key (Readingguideforoutofhospitalguidelines2026KEY.docx) words the beta-blocker follow-up as calls at appropriate intervals for up to 12–24 hours based on the judgment of the poison center.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 guidelines; Readingguideforoutofhospitalguidelines2026KEY.docx; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-065", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"apply", readingId:"aapcc-ccb",
 concept:"self-harm-counseling", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The overdose appears to have been an attempt at self-harm. Beyond treating the poisoning, what does the guideline call for?",
 options:[
  {t:"Referral to the emergency department, then to mental health care", correct:true, why:"Her key: refer the patient to the ED, then referral to mental health care. On 10/19 she said they will need counseling and evaluation, and on 10/20 that they need to get to mental health care as well."},
  {t:"A longer course of activated charcoal", correct:false, why:"Charcoal has nothing to do with intent."},
  {t:"Automatic dialysis for 24 hours", correct:false, why:"Dialysis is for severe poisonings, not intent."},
  {t:"Nothing beyond treating the poisoning", correct:false, why:"She called the whole-patient point very important."},
  {t:"A second guideline document specific to self-harm, applied in place of the drug-class document", correct:false, why:"The self-harm step is part of the same beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker documents: the poisoning is treated, and the patient is referred to the ED and then to mental health care."}],
 teach:"Her key: refer the patient to the emergency department (ED), then to mental health care. For either drug class, if the patient was trying to harm themselves, treating the poisoning is not enough: they need help, counseling and evaluation, so that whatever is going on in their lives gets addressed. She flagged this as a very important point in the guidance documents: treat the whole patient.",
 quote:"If you think they were trying to harm themselves, what do you need to do as well? It's not just treating the poisoning, right? They're gonna need some help. They're gonna need some counseling, some evaluation, make sure that whatever's going on in their lives, that they could get some help with that, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 guidelines; Readingguideforoutofhospitalguidelines2026KEY.docx; transcript 10/19; transcript 10/20"},

{id:"L16-066", dupOf:"L16-058", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"guideline", skill:"apply", type:"match", readingId:"aapcc-ccb",
 concept:"formulation-window-table", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Match each formulation with the window after ingestion within which an asymptomatic patient must still go to the hospital.",
 left:["Immediate-release calcium channel blocker, or beta blocker other than sotalol", "Sustained-release beta blocker", "Modified-release calcium channel blocker other than verapamil", "Modified-release verapamil"],
 right:["6 hours", "8 hours", "18 hours", "24 hours"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Immediate-release calcium channel blocker, or beta blocker other than sotalol", r:"6 hours", why:"The same for both classes; her key excepts sotalol."},
  {l:"Sustained-release beta blocker", r:"8 hours", why:"The beta-blocker guideline's extended-release window."},
  {l:"Modified-release calcium channel blocker other than verapamil", r:"18 hours", why:"The calcium channel blocker guideline's extended-release window."},
  {l:"Modified-release verapamil", r:"24 hours", why:"Verapamil is really different."}],
 teach:"Immediate-release tablets are absorbed quickly, so by 6 hours a patient who is going to be ill should show symptoms; that 6-hour window is shared by both classes. Extended-release products clump and can break apart unpredictably, so their windows are longer and differ by class, following the case data: 8 hours for sustained-release beta blockers, 18 for modified-release calcium channel blockers, and 24 for modified-release verapamil, which has multiple mechanisms.",
 quote:"I want you to know those time frames, OK, which drug formulation is OK at this time frame. If it's extended release calcium channel blocker, not verapamil, what's the time frame, OK? What's the time frame for a beta blocker extended release, OK? So, that's what you need to know. Also, you need to know you don't make them barf at home, it's too dangerous, OK?",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Discussion of OD Guidelines’; AAPCC 2005 guidelines; Readingguideforoutofhospitalguidelines2026KEY.docx; transcript 10/19"},

/* ---------------- The poisoning treatment table ---------------- */
{id:"L16-067", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"table", skill:"recall", readingId:"dipiro-e9",
 concept:"table-sources", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which two sources were recommended for filling in the poisoning treatment table?",
 options:[
  {t:"DiPiro 13th edition chapter e9, the section on treating specific poisonings, and the Marraffa article", correct:true, why:"The next section of the chapter after the one read last week, and the Marraffa antidotes article; the key is posted after the table is turned in."},
  {t:"The Golan pharmacology textbook, whose figures she used, together with its chapter text on each poisoning", correct:false, why:"Golan supplied the figures; she said its text is too detailed for what the class needs and it is not a required book."},
  {t:"The full texts of the beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker guidelines and their appendices", correct:false, why:"Only the abstracts were assigned, for the guideline discussion."},
  {t:"A web search of poison center sites and the FDA labels of each antidote", correct:false, why:"Not the sources she named; all the information is in the chapter and the article."},
  {t:"An AI tool, provided the output is checked against the DiPiro chapter afterwards", correct:false, why:"\"Don't use AI please. Make your brain do the work.\""}],
 teach:"The poisoning treatment table, due the next morning and discussed at the start of class, is a study tool for the exam. The sources are DiPiro's Pharmacotherapy, 13th edition, chapter e9, the section on treating specific poisonings (the part after the general approach read on 10/14), and the Marraffa antidotes article, which she called the quickest read. Bulleted entries, no AI, work with friends; her key is posted after everyone has turned the table in and shows the detail she expects.",
 quote:"I really think the best places for you to go to would be your Depiro's, uh, the 13th edition, chapter E9. We already looked at part of that chapter last week, but you would go to the next section of the chapter where we're actually, they're actually talking about treating specific poisoning. So, that will be very helpful for filling in your poisoning treatment table. And then also this article by Marafa.",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Treating specific poisonings Table’; DiPiro ch. e9; Marraffa 2012; transcript 10/19"},

{id:"L16-068", lecture:"L16", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L16", sub:"table", skill:"recall", readingId:"marraffa",
 concept:"marraffa-article-content", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"What does the Marraffa article (Antidotes for toxicological emergencies) give, and why was it recommended?",
 options:[
  {t:"Symptoms, treatments and their mechanisms, and it is the quickest read", correct:true, why:"It breaks down step by step the symptoms seen in these overdoses, the treatments used and, importantly, their mechanisms of action, faster than the DiPiro chapter."},
  {t:"The out-of-hospital triage windows for every drug class with a guideline", correct:false, why:"Those are the AAPCC guidelines."},
  {t:"The structures of the chelating agents to be memorized", correct:false, why:"Structures are not to be memorized; the agent-to-metal table is."},
  {t:"A ranking of the level of evidence behind each antidote", correct:false, why:"Grading of evidence is the guideline documents' feature."},
  {t:"The pharmacogenomics of warfarin dosing", correct:false, why:"That comes in the pharmacogenomics lectures."}],
 teach:"The Marraffa article is a practical review of antidotes. She recommended it for the poisoning table because it breaks down, step by step, the symptoms of each overdose, the treatments and their mechanisms of action, which are the columns of the table, and because it is the fastest of the two sources to read. The article is tested only through what she discussed.",
 quote:"It's a really good article and it breaks down step by step, a lot of the, the symptoms that you'll see in these overdoses and then the treatments that we use, and importantly, their mechanism of action. OK? ... I think the Marafa article has the information in it, um, fast. It's the quickest to read through compared to the Dipiro article, OK.",
 note:"The transcript renders Marraffa as \"Marafa\" and \"Marrafa\".",
 cite:"Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Treating specific poisonings Table’; Marraffa 2012 (Am J Health-Syst Pharm 69:199); transcript 10/19"}
);
